var e=JSON.parse('[[1101,"Caching can be implemented by means of an object or a `Map`, saving the data by a key and returning it on repeated requests. This lowers the number of repeated computations or requests to the server.","Caching is the saving of the results of work for reuse.\\nThe main steps of the implementation:\\n\\n- The choice of the data structure\\n\\n- An `Object` or a `Map` for simple cases.\\n\\n- The logic of the saving\\nOn the first request the result is computed and saved.\\n\\n- The logic of the retrieval\\nOn a repeated request the result is taken from the cache.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nCaching can be implemented in JS without libraries, using a `Map` or an `Object`, which raises performance on repeated requests.","function createCache() {\\n  const cache = new Map();\\n  return function(key, computeFn) {\\n    if (cache.has(key)) {\\n      return cache.get(key);\\n    }\\n    const result = computeFn();\\n    cache.set(key, result);\\n    return result;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst getData = createCache();\\n\\nconsole.log(getData(\'user\', () => ({ name: \'Alice\' })));\\nconsole.log(getData(\'user\', () => ({ name: \'Bob\' }))); // Will take it from the cache"],[1102,"For large volumes of data it is better to use `IndexedDB`, since it supports asynchronous working and the holding of structured objects. `LocalStorage` is suitable for small volumes (up to ~5 MB) and synchronous operations.","A comparison:\\n\\n- LocalStorage\\n\\n- The volume: ~5 MB.\\n\\n- The API: synchronous, it blocks the main thread.\\n\\n- The format of the storage: strings.\\n\\n- It is suitable for small settings and tokens.\\n\\n- IndexedDB\\n\\n- The volume: hundreds of MB.\\n\\n- The API: asynchronous, it does not block the UI.\\n\\n- The format of the storage: objects, binary data.\\n\\n- It is suitable for offline applications and large collections of data.\\n\\nAn example of work with IndexedDB:\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`IndexedDB` is the choice for large volumes of data and complex structures, `LocalStorage` for small and fast saves.","const request = indexedDB.open(\'myDB\', 1);\\n\\nrequest.onupgradeneeded = e => {\\n  const db = e.target.result;\\n  db.createObjectStore(\'users\', { keyPath: \'id\' });\\n};\\n\\nrequest.onsuccess = e => {\\n  const db = e.target.result;\\n  const tx = db.transaction(\'users\', \'readwrite\');\\n  tx.objectStore(\'users\').add({ id: 1, name: \'Alice\' });\\n};"],[1103,"You can limit the size of the cache and delete the old entries on the LRU (Least Recently Used) principle or by a lifetime (TTL). It also matters to clear the cache periodically.","The strategies:\\n\\n- The limitation of the size\\n\\n- Hold a maximum of N entries.\\n\\n- On an excess, delete the oldest ones.\\n\\n- The lifetime (TTL)\\n\\n- Every entry has a term of validity.\\n\\n- The deletion of outdated data when it is requested.\\n\\n- The clearing on events\\n\\n- Clear the cache when the application\'s version changes.\\n\\nAn example of an LRU cache:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nControl of the size and the lifetime of a cache prevents its overflow and raises its efficiency.","class LRUCache {\\n  constructor(limit) {\\n    this.limit = limit;\\n    this.map = new Map();\\n  }\\n  get(key) {\\n    if (!this.map.has(key)) return null;\\n    const value = this.map.get(key);\\n    this.map.delete(key);\\n    this.map.set(key, value);\\n    return value;\\n  }\\n  set(key, value) {\\n    if (this.map.size >= this.limit) {\\n      const oldest = this.map.keys().next().value;\\n      this.map.delete(oldest);\\n    }\\n    this.map.set(key, value);\\n  }\\n}"],[1104,"The `map` method creates a new array, applying the function passed to it to every element of the original array. It can be implemented by means of a loop.","The `map` method in JavaScript:\\n\\n- It does not change the original array.\\n\\n- It returns a new array of the same length.\\n\\n- It calls the callback for every element.\\n\\nAn example of the implementation:\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`map` is convenient for the transformation of arrays, and its implementation shows an understanding of the principles of the working of higher-order methods.","function myMap(array, callback) {\\n  const result = [];\\n  for (let i = 0; i < array.length; i++) {\\n    result.push(callback(array[i], i, array));\\n  }\\n  return result;\\n}\\n\\n// The use\\nconst nums = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst doubled = myMap(nums, x => x * 2);\\nconsole.log(doubled); // [2, 4, 6]"],[1105,"The Liskov substitution principle states that any child class should correctly replace its parent without a disruption of the program\'s working. In React this means that a component extending or replacing another one should fully preserve its contract (the props, the behaviour), so as not to break the application\'s logic.","The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) is one of the parts of SOLID, and it is formulated like this:\\nThe objects of child classes should be interchangeable with the objects of the base class without a violation of the program\'s correctness.\\nThe application in React:\\n\\n- The contract of the props\\n\\n- A new component replacing an old one should accept the same mandatory props and interpret them identically.\\n\\n- The behaviour\\n\\n- The replacement should not change the expected behaviour for the rest of the system.\\n\\n- An example\\n\\nA violation of the LSP:\\n\\n- The new component requires additional mandatory props that the parent did not require.\\n\\n- The new component changes the meaning of the props that already exist.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nOn the front end the LSP helps you design reusable and replaceable components, which especially matters for large projects with long-term maintenance.","function Button({ onClick, label }) {\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;\\n}\\n\\nfunction IconButton(props) {\\n  return <Button {...props} />;\\n}\\n// IconButton can be substituted in place of Button — the contract is observed"],[1106,"An application\'s structure should ensure the single responsibility of the modules, loose coupling, the ability to substitute implementations and ease of extension. In React this is achieved by the separation of the UI components, the business logic and the infrastructure.","SOLID on the front end:\\n\\n- S — Single Responsibility\\n\\n- One component = one responsibility.\\n\\n- A UI component should contain no business logic.\\n\\n- O — Open/Closed\\n\\n- New behaviour is added by extension rather than by a change in the existing code.\\n\\n- An example: the addition of new pages through the router rather than the rewriting of App.js.\\n\\n- L — Liskov Substitution\\n\\n- Components and functions can be replaced without a change in the rest of the code.\\n\\n- I — Interface Segregation\\n\\n- Do not overload components with props that they do not need.\\n\\n- D — Dependency Inversion\\n\\n- Components depend on abstractions rather than on concrete implementations.\\n\\nAn example of a structure:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe application of SOLID on the front end raises an application\'s readability, testability and scalability.","src/\\n  components/   // The UI components\\n  hooks/        // The logic of the states\\n  services/     // The API, the stores\\n  pages/        // The pages\\n  store/        // The state management"],[1107,"The main problems are the difficulty of synchronising dependencies, the management of versions, the isolation of styles, the exchange of state and the integration of different technologies.","The main difficulties:\\n\\n- A version conflict of the dependencies\\n\\n- Different micro front ends may use different versions of React or of libraries.\\n\\n- The isolation of the styles\\n\\n- Conflicts of CSS are possible with global styles.\\n\\n- Performance\\n\\n- The loading of identical libraries in every micro front end.\\n\\n- The exchange of state\\n\\n- The need for a shared data bus or the synchronisation of events.\\n\\n- The build and the deployment\\n\\n- A different CI/CD pipeline for every micro front end.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nMicro front ends give flexibility, but they complicate the architecture and require strict discipline in the management of the dependencies and the styles.",null],[1108,"A single registry of versions or the loading of the shared dependencies through a CDN or module federation is usually used. It matters to avoid the duplication of large libraries in every micro front end.","The approaches:\\n\\n- Shared dependencies\\n\\n- In Module Federation (Webpack 5) React can be moved out as `shared`.\\n\\n- A CDN\\n\\n- The loading of the dependencies globally and their use in every micro front end.\\n\\n- A monorepo\\n\\n- A shared code base with a single `package.json`.\\n\\n- Lock files\\n\\n- A single lock file for every micro front end.\\n\\nAn example of Module Federation:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nA single version of the dependencies lowers the weight of the bundles and prevents bugs caused by the incompatibility of APIs.","// host webpack config\\nshared: {\\n  react: { singleton: true, requiredVersion: \'^18.0.0\' },\\n  \'react-dom\': { singleton: true }\\n}"],[1109,"The isolation is achieved through separate bundles, scoped CSS, a namespace for the global objects and the use of the Shadow DOM when it is needed.","The ways of isolating:\\n\\n- CSS modules or CSS-in-JS\\n\\n- The generation of unique classes.\\n\\n- The Shadow DOM\\n\\n- The complete isolation of the styles inside a Web Component.\\n\\n- Module Federation / an iframe\\n\\n- The complete loading of an application with its own environment.\\n\\n- A namespace\\n\\n- The separation of the global variables.\\n\\nAn example of CSS modules:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nIsolation prevents conflicts between micro front ends and makes a system more robust.","/* Button.module.css */\\n.button {\\n  background: red;\\n}\\n\\nimport styles from \'./Button.module.css\';\\n<button className={styles.button}>Click</button>"],[1110,"A higher-order function:\\n\\n- Takes another function as an argument.\\n\\n- Returns a function.\\nExamples: `map`, `filter`, `setTimeout`.","Higher-order functions let you abstract operations away.\\nExample 1: It takes a function\\n\\nExample 2: It returns a function\\n\\nWhere is it applied:\\n\\n- The handling of arrays (`map`, `filter`).\\n\\n- Asynchronous operations (`setTimeout`).\\n\\n- Closures.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nHigher-order functions make code flexible and reusable.","function greet(name, callback) {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);\\n  callback();\\n}\\n\\ngreet(\\"Alice\\", () => console.log(\\"Callback called!\\"));\\n\\nfunction multiplier(factor) {\\n  return function(number) {\\n    return number * factor;\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst double = multiplier(2);\\nconsole.log(double(5)); // 10"],[1111,"- GET obtains data (the parameters in the URL, it is cached).\\n\\n- POST sends data (the body of the request, it is not cached).\\n\\n- GET is less secure; POST is for changing data.","1. GET:\\n\\n- It is used for the reading of data.\\n\\n- The parameters are passed in the URL (`?key=value`).\\n\\n- It is limited in length (it depends on the browser).\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n2. POST:\\n\\n- It is used for the sending of data (logins, forms).\\n\\n- The data is in the body of the request (it is not visible in the URL).\\n\\n- There is no limitation on the length.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- GET is for safe requests (a search, filtering).\\n\\n- POST is for the creation or the changing of data.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe choice of the method depends on the type of the operation.","fetch(\'/api/users?id=1\');\\n\\nfetch(\'/api/users\', {\\n  method: \'POST\',\\n  body: JSON.stringify({ name: \'Alice\' })\\n});"],[1112,"- A proxy server (on the back end).\\n\\n- JSONP (an outdated way).\\n\\n- The configuration of CORS on the server (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin`).\\n\\n- The use of extensions for developers (only for testing).","1. A proxy server:\\n\\n- The request goes through your server, which has no CORS limitations.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n2. JSONP:\\n\\n- It works only for GET requests.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\n3. The configuration of the server:\\n\\n- Add the headers:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe best way is the configuration of CORS on the server, or a proxy.","// Your back end makes the request to someone else\'s API\\nfetch(\'/your-proxy?url=https://external-api.com/data\');\\n\\nfunction handleResponse(data) {\\n  console.log(data);\\n}\\nconst script = document.createElement(\'script\');\\nscript.src = \'https://external-api.com/data?callback=handleResponse\';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(script);\\n\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: *"],[1113,"A reference type is objects (arrays and functions included), which are passed by reference rather than by value. A change in a copy changes the original.","An example:\\n\\nHow to avoid the problems:\\n\\n- Use `Object.assign` or the spread operator for the copying:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nObjects are passed by reference, primitives by value.","const a = { value: 1 };\\nconst b = a; // b refers to the same object\\nb.value = 2;\\nconsole.log(a.value); // 2 (the original has changed)\\n\\nconst a = { value: 1 };\\nconst b = { ...a }; // A copy\\nb.value = 2;\\nconsole.log(a.value); // 1 (the original has not changed)"],[1114,"- Currying:\\n\\n`function add(a) {\\nreturn function(b) { return a + b; };\\n}`\\n\\n- The addition of a method to the prototype of a number:\\n\\n`Number.prototype.add = function(b) { return this + b; };`","1. Currying:\\nThe function returns a function until it has received every argument.\\n\\n2. The extension of the prototype:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nCurrying is useful for the partial application of functions. Prototypes should be extended with care.","function add(a) {\\n  return function(b) {\\n    return a + b;\\n  };\\n}\\nconsole.log(add(1)(2)); // 3\\n\\nNumber.prototype.add = function(b) {\\n  return this + b;\\n};\\nconsole.log((5).add(3)); // 8"],[1115,"Arrow functions have no `this` of their own — they take it from the outer context. They are therefore not suitable for the methods of an object or for constructors.","An example of the problem:\\n\\nThe solution:\\nUse ordinary functions for methods:\\n\\nWhere arrow functions are useful:\\n\\n- Callbacks (they preserve the context):\\n\\nConclusion:\\nArrow functions are convenient for callbacks but not for methods.","const obj = {\\n  value: 10,\\n  print: () => console.log(this.value) // undefined (this is the global object)\\n};\\nobj.print();\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  value: 10,\\n  print() { console.log(this.value); } // 10\\n};\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(this), 1000);"],[1116,"In ordinary functions `this` inside `setInterval`/`setTimeout` is lost (it becomes `window`, or `undefined` in strict mode). Arrow functions preserve the `this` from the outer context.","An example of the problem:\\n\\nSolution 1: An arrow function\\n\\nSolution 2: The binding of the context\\n\\nConclusion:\\nArrow functions or `bind` help you preserve `this`.","const obj = {\\n  value: 10,\\n  print() {\\n    setTimeout(function() {\\n      console.log(this.value); // undefined (this is the global object)\\n    }, 1000);\\n  }\\n};\\nobj.print();\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  value: 10,\\n  print() {\\n    setTimeout(() => console.log(this.value), 1000); // 10\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nsetTimeout(function() { console.log(this.value); }.bind(obj), 1000);"],[1117,"`var` has function scope rather than block scope. In a loop every iteration uses one and the same variable, so in the closures you get the last value.","An example of the problem:\\n\\nSolution 1: `let` (block scope)\\n\\nSolution 2: A closure\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`let` and `const` solve the problem, since they have block scope.","for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); // 3, 3, 3\\n}\\n\\nfor (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 1000); // 0, 1, 2\\n}\\n\\nfor (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\\n  (function(j) {\\n    setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 1000); // 0, 1, 2\\n  })(i);\\n}"],[1118,"- `map` creates a new array, applying a function to every element of the original.\\n\\n- `filter` creates a new array, leaving in only the elements that satisfy a condition.\\n\\n- Neither method changes the original array (they are pure functions).","1. `map`:\\n\\n- It takes a callback function, which transforms every element.\\n\\n- It returns a new array of the same length.\\n\\n2. `filter`:\\n\\n- The callback returns `true`/`false` for every element.\\n\\n- Only the elements where it is `true` land in the new array.\\n\\nThe original array:\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`map` and `filter` return new arrays; the original data remains unchanged.","const numbers = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst doubled = numbers.map(x => x * 2); // [2, 4, 6]\\n\\nconst evens = numbers.filter(x => x % 2 === 0); // [2]\\n\\nconsole.log(numbers); // [1, 2, 3] (it has not changed)"],[1119,"If no element passes the condition, `filter` will return an empty array (`[]`).","An example:\\n\\nWhy not `null` or `undefined`?\\n\\n- This agrees with the logic of the working of the array methods: an array is always returned.\\n\\n- An empty array is a valid result, with which work can be continued (other methods can be called on it, for example).\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`filter` always returns an array, even an empty one.","const numbers = [1, 3, 5];\\nconst evens = numbers.filter(x => x % 2 === 0); // []"],[1120,"- The primitives: `number`, `string`, `boolean`, `null`, `undefined`, `symbol`, `bigint`. They are held by value.\\n\\n- The reference types: `object`, `array`, `function`. They are held by reference.","1. The primitives:\\n\\n- They are copied by value.\\n\\n- They are immutable (strings, for example).\\n\\n2. The reference types:\\n\\n- The reference to the object is copied.\\n\\n- They are mutable.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nPrimitives work by value, reference types by reference.","let a = 5;\\nlet b = a; // The value is copied\\nb = 10;\\nconsole.log(a); // 5 (it has not changed)\\n\\nlet obj1 = { name: \'Alice\' };\\nlet obj2 = obj1; // The reference is copied\\nobj2.name = \'Bob\';\\nconsole.log(obj1.name); // \'Bob\' (the original has changed)"],[1121,"A promise can be in one of three states:\\n\\n- `pending` — waiting.\\n\\n- `fulfilled` — carried out successfully.\\n\\n- `rejected` — carried out with an error.","1. `pending`:\\n\\n- The initial state, while the promise has not finished.\\n\\n2. `fulfilled`:\\n\\n- The promise has finished successfully (`resolve` has been called).\\n\\n3. `rejected`:\\n\\n- The promise has finished with an error (`reject` has been called).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nA promise passes from `pending` into `fulfilled` or `rejected` once and once only.","const promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => resolve(\'Done!\'), 1000);\\n});\\n\\npromise\\n  .then(result => console.log(result)) // \'Done!\'\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));"],[1122,"The second argument of `then` is the analogue of `catch`:\\n\\n`promise.then(\\nresult => console.log(result),\\nerror => console.error(error) // The handling of the error\\n);`","A comparison with `catch`:\\n\\nThe difference:\\n\\n- `catch` intercepts the errors from every preceding `then`.\\n\\n- The second argument of `then` only takes the errors of the current promise.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nBoth ways work, but `catch` is usually preferable for a chain of promises.","// Variant 1: catch\\npromise\\n  .then(result => console.log(result))\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));\\n\\n// Variant 2: then with two arguments\\npromise.then(\\n  result => console.log(result),\\n  error => console.error(error)\\n);"],[1123,"Use an `IntersectionObserver` for the efficient tracking of visibility. The alternative is the comparison of the element\'s coordinates with the dimensions of the viewport.","1. An IntersectionObserver (the modern way):\\n\\n2. The checking of the coordinates (the outdated way):\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAn `IntersectionObserver` is the preferable method because of its performance and its simplicity.","const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      console.log(\'The element is in the zone of visibility\');\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\n\\nobserver.observe(document.querySelector(\'.element\'));\\n\\nfunction isInViewport(element) {\\n  const rect = element.getBoundingClientRect();\\n  return (\\n    rect.top >= 0 &&\\n    rect.left >= 0 &&\\n    rect.bottom <= window.innerHeight &&\\n    rect.right <= window.innerWidth\\n  );\\n}"],[1124,"The choice depends on the task:\\n\\n- UI libraries (Material UI, Bootstrap) are for fast development and standard components.\\n\\n- Custom CSS is for a unique design and complete control.","The pluses of UI libraries:\\n\\n- Ready-made components (buttons, forms).\\n\\n- Responsiveness \\"out of the box\\".\\n\\n- A consistent design.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- Limited customisation.\\n\\n- Superfluous code if not every component is used.\\n\\nWhen to write the CSS yourself:\\n\\n- A non-standard design.\\n\\n- The need for optimisation (a minimal bundle).\\n\\n- Projects with strict requirements of performance.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- The libraries: Material UI, Ant Design, Bootstrap.\\n\\n- Custom CSS: Tailwind (the utility approach), Sass.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFor an MVP or for corporate products, libraries. For unique interfaces, custom CSS.",null],[1125,"First `&&` (logical AND) is carried out, then `||` (logical OR). The order:\\n\\n- `B && C` is evaluated.\\n\\n- The result is compared with `A` through `||`.","The priority of the operators:\\n\\n- `&&` has a higher priority than `||` (like multiplication before addition in mathematics).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- If `B && C` is equal to `true`, then `result = A || true` will return `A` (if `A` is truthy) or `true`.\\n\\n- If `B && C` is equal to `false`, then `result = A || false` will return `A`.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nUse brackets to set the order explicitly: `(A || B) && C` or `A || (B && C)`","const result = A || B && C;\\n// Analogously:\\nconst temp = B && C;\\nconst result = A || temp;"],[1126,"`async/await` makes asynchronous code:\\n\\n- Readable (it resembles synchronous code).\\n\\n- Convenient for debugging (the call stack is not \\"torn apart\\").\\n\\n- Easy in the handling of errors (through `try/catch`).","A comparison of the styles:\\n\\n- A Promise:\\n\\n- async/await:\\n\\nThe advantages of async/await:\\n\\n- There is no \\"hellish\\" nesting (a `then` inside a `then`).\\n\\n- The errors are caught by the standard `try/catch`.\\n\\n- It simplifies work with loops (`for`, `map`).\\n\\nWhen to use Promises:\\n\\n- Parallel requests through `Promise.all()`.\\n\\n- Work with libraries that return a Promise.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`async/await` is syntactic sugar over Promise, which makes code cleaner.","fetchData()\\n  .then(data => process(data))\\n  .catch(error => console.error(error));\\n\\ntry {\\n  const data = await fetchData();\\n  process(data);\\n} catch (error) {\\n  console.error(error);\\n}"],[1127,"Use the `addEventListener` method to subscribe to the `click` event:\\n\\n`document.querySelector(\'button\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\nconsole.log(\'The button has been pressed!\');\\n});`","The ways of subscribing:\\n\\n- Through `addEventListener` (the recommended way):\\n\\n- Through an HTML attribute (an outdated method):\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- `addEventListener` lets you add several handlers for one event.\\n\\n- To remove a handler, use `removeEventListener`.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAlways prefer `addEventListener` for the flexibility and the better maintainability of the code.","const button = document.getElementById(\'myButton\');\\nbutton.addEventListener(\'click\', handleClick);\\nfunction handleClick() {\\n  alert(\'A click!\');\\n}\\n\\n<button onclick=\\"alert(\'A click!\')\\">Press me</button>"],[1128,"Pass a third argument of `true` to `addEventListener` to subscribe to the capture phase:\\n\\n`element.addEventListener(\'click\', handler, true);`","The phases of an event:\\n\\n- Capture: The event goes from the top down (from `window` to the target element).\\n\\n- Target: The event has reached the element.\\n\\n- Bubble: The event rises upwards (the default).\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe capture phase is useful for intercepting events before they reach the target element (for modal windows, for example).","document.querySelector(\'div\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n  console.log(\'Capture\');\\n}, true);\\n\\ndocument.querySelector(\'button\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n  console.log(\'Bubble\');\\n});"],[1129,"Add the headers to the server\'s response:\\n\\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, OPTIONS\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type`","The minimal configuration (a Node.js example):\\n\\nFor requests with cookies or authorisation:\\n\\nConclusion:\\nCORS is configured on the server. Use `*` for development, but limit the domains in production.","app.use((req, res, next) => {\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Origin\', \'*\'); // Or a specific domain\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Methods\', \'GET, POST, OPTIONS\');\\n  res.setHeader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Headers\', \'Content-Type, Authorization\');\\n  next();\\n});\\n\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://yourdomain.com\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true"],[1130,"- The storage: In the browser (you can look at them in DevTools → Application → Cookies).\\n\\n- The passing: They are automatically attached to HTTP requests in the `Cookie` header.","1. The setting of a cookie (the server):\\n\\n2. The setting of a cookie (JavaScript):\\n\\n3. The reading of cookies:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- Cookies are tied to a domain and a path.\\n\\n- The maximum size is ~4 KB per domain.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nCookies are a simple way of holding data between requests, but for large volumes of data use `localStorage`.","Set-Cookie: sessionId=abc123; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure\\n\\ndocument.cookie = \\"username=John; expires=Thu, 18 Dec 2025 12:00:00 UTC; path=/\\";\\n\\nconsole.log(document.cookie); // \\"username=John; sessionId=abc123\\""],[1131,"The main flags:\\n\\n- `HttpOnly`: Forbids access to the cookie through JavaScript (protection against XSS).\\n\\n- `Secure`: The passing only over HTTPS.\\n\\n- `SameSite`: Protection against CSRF (`Strict`, `Lax`, `None`).\\n\\n- `Expires`/`Max-Age`: The cookie\'s lifetime.","An example of a secure cookie:\\n\\nThe purpose of the flags:\\n\\n- `HttpOnly`: It cannot be stolen through `document.cookie`.\\n\\n- `Secure`: The cookie will not go over HTTP (only HTTPS).\\n\\n- `SameSite=Strict`: The cookie will not be sent on a navigation from another site.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nAlways use `HttpOnly` and `Secure` for critical data (tokens, for example).","Set-Cookie: token=xyz; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict; Path=/"],[1132,"The access token is held in memory or in LocalStorage for access to the API. The refresh token is held in an HttpOnly cookie for a secure refresh. The token is automatically added to the headers of the requests and is refreshed when it expires.","The scheme of the working:\\n\\n- The user enters their credentials\\n\\n- The server returns the access and refresh tokens\\n\\n- The access token is held in the client\\n\\n- The refresh token is held in an HttpOnly cookie\\n\\n- On the expiry of the access token it is automatically refreshed\\n\\nSecure storage:\\n\\n- The access token: localStorage or memory\\n\\n- The refresh token: an HttpOnly cookie\\n\\n- Protection against CSRF and XSS attacks\\n\\nThe automatic refresh:\\n\\nBest practices:\\n\\n- A short lifetime for the access token\\n\\n- The Secure and HttpOnly flags for the cookies\\n\\n- The validation of the tokens on the server\\n\\n- Protection against replay attacks","// An interceptor for axios\\naxios.interceptors.response.use(\\n  response => response,\\n  async error => {\\n    if (error.response.status === 401) {\\n      const newToken = await refreshToken();\\n      // The repeat of the request with the new token\\n    }\\n    return Promise.reject(error);\\n  }\\n);"],[1133,"To integrate maps, use the JavaScript API of a cartographic service (Google Maps, Yandex Maps, Leaflet). Initialise the map in the component, add the markers and the event handlers.","The popular solutions:\\n\\n- The Google Maps JavaScript API\\n\\n- The Yandex Maps API\\n\\n- Leaflet (open-source)\\n\\n- Mapbox GL JS\\n\\nThe basic integration:\\n\\nThe React integration:\\nUse wrapper libraries:\\n\\n- `@react-google-maps/api`\\n\\n- `react-yandex-maps`\\n\\n- `react-leaflet`\\n\\nThe key functions:\\n\\n- The addition of markers\\n\\n- The drawing of shapes and zones\\n\\n- The geocoding of addresses\\n\\n- Routing and directions\\n\\n- Custom styles for the maps\\n\\nThe optimisation:\\n\\n- The lazy loading of the maps\\n\\n- The clustering of the markers\\n\\n- The optimisation of performance\\n\\n- The caching of the requests","<div id=\\"map\\" style=\\"width: 100%; height: 400px;\\"></div>\\n\\n<script>\\n  const map = new google.maps.Map(document.getElementById(\'map\'), {\\n    center: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    zoom: 10\\n  });\\n  \\n  new google.maps.Marker({\\n    position: { lat: 55.76, lng: 37.64 },\\n    map: map,\\n    title: \'Hello World!\'\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[1134,"For drawing on the web, use the Canvas API for pixel graphics or SVG for vector ones. The popular libraries: D3.js for complex visualisations, Fabric.js for interactive drawings, Three.js for 3D graphics.","The technologies of drawing:\\n\\n- Canvas: pixel graphics, high performance\\n\\n- SVG: vector graphics, scaling without loss\\n\\n- WebGL: 3D graphics through Canvas\\n\\nThe popular libraries:\\n\\n- D3.js: complex data visualisations\\n\\n- Fabric.js: interactive drawings and editors\\n\\n- Three.js: 3D graphics and animations\\n\\n- Konva.js: 2D graphics for React\\n\\nAn example of the use of Canvas:\\n\\nThe React integration:\\n\\nThe application:\\n\\n- Dashboards and analytics\\n\\n- Graphics editors\\n\\n- Interactive infographics\\n\\n- Games and simulations\\n\\nBest practices:\\n\\n- The optimisation of performance\\n\\n- Responsiveness for different devices\\n\\n- Accessibility for users\\n\\n- The caching of complex computations","const canvas = document.getElementById(\'myCanvas\');\\nconst ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');\\n\\nctx.fillStyle = \'red\';\\nctx.fillRect(10, 10, 100, 100);\\n\\nimport { Stage, Layer, Circle } from \'react-konva\';\\n\\nfunction DrawingApp() {\\n  return (\\n    <Stage width={window.innerWidth} height={window.innerHeight}>\\n      <Layer>\\n        <Circle x={100} y={100} radius={50} fill=\\"red\\" />\\n      </Layer>\\n    </Stage>\\n  );\\n}"],[1135,"A reflow takes place when a page\'s structure or the dimensions of elements change and the browser has to recompute the layout. A repaint is caused when the appearance changes (the colour, for example) but the dimensions remain as they were. Frequent reflows slow a page\'s working down, so an effort is made to minimise them — by grouping the changes in the DOM or using `classList` rather than changing the styles one at a time, for example.","What a Reflow and a Repaint are\\n\\n- A reflow is the recomputation of the geometry of the elements: the dimensions, the positions, the structure. On a change in a block\'s width or the addition of a new element, for example.\\n\\n- A repaint is the repainting of the appearance without the recomputation of the positions. A change of the background or of the text\'s colour, for example.\\n\\nThe actions that cause a Reflow\\n\\n- A change in an element\'s dimensions (`width`, `height`, `margin`, `padding`).\\n\\n- The addition or the deletion of nodes in the DOM.\\n\\n- The use of methods such as `offsetWidth` and `getComputedStyle` (they force the browser to compute the current state).\\n\\n- A change of the fonts or of the text\'s content.\\n\\nHow to avoid frequent Reflows\\n\\n- Group several changes together:\\n\\n- Use `classList.add/remove` rather than changing every style separately.\\n\\n- Work with the element outside the DOM (through a `documentFragment`) and then insert it whole.\\n\\n- Use `requestAnimationFrame` for animations.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA reflow is an expensive operation, and it is worth minimising it when working with dynamic content. The less the browser has to recompute the layout of the elements, the faster the page works.","element.style.cssText = \\"width: 100px; height: 100px; color: red;\\";"],[1136,"Deep copying can be implemented by means of a recursive function, which manually creates a new object or array and copies all of its properties. For every property, if it is an object, the function calls itself. The main difficulties: the handling of circular references (when an object refers to itself), the copying of special objects (such as Date, Map and Set) and support for various data types.","The implementation of deep copying \\"by hand\\" requires a thorough traversal of every property of the original object and the correct copying of every value.\\nThe basic implementation of a recursive function:\\n\\nThe difficulties that can arise:\\n\\n- Circular references: If object A refers to object B and B refers to A, a naive recursive function will enter an infinite loop and cause a stack overflow. To solve this problem a `WeakMap` data structure is used for remembering the objects already copied.\\n\\n- \\"Fragile\\" objects: Some objects, such as `Date`, `Map`, `Set` and `RegExp`, will lose their internal structure and functionality on the simple copying of their properties. They have to be created anew by means of their constructors.\\n\\n- Performance: The recursive traversal of large and complex objects can be slow.\\n\\n- Non-enumerable properties and getters/setters: The basic implementation with `for...in` copies only the enumerable properties. It does not copy property descriptors (getters/setters) or properties from the chain of prototypes.\\n\\nConclusion:\\nA manual implementation is useful for an understanding of the language\'s mechanisms, but in production it is better to use the built-in methods (`StructuredClone`) or proven libraries (`lodash.cloneDeep`, for example), which already solve all of these difficulties.","function deepClone(value, visited = new WeakMap()) {\\n  // 1. The handling of primitives and null/undefined\\n  if (value === null || typeof value !== \'object\') {\\n    return value;\\n  }\\n\\n  // 2. The handling of circular references\\n  if (visited.has(value)) {\\n    return visited.get(value);\\n  }\\n\\n  // 3. The handling of the special built-in objects\\n  if (value instanceof Date) {\\n    return new Date(value);\\n  }\\n  if (value instanceof Map) {\\n    const copy = new Map();\\n    visited.set(value, copy);\\n    value.forEach((val, key) => {\\n      copy.set(key, deepClone(val, visited));\\n    });\\n    return copy;\\n  }\\n  // Analogously for Set, RegExp and the others...\\n\\n  // 4. The handling of arrays and ordinary objects\\n  const copy = Array.isArray(value) ? [] : {};\\n  visited.set(value, copy); // We save the copy for the checking of circular references\\n\\n  // The recursive copying of every property\\n  for (let key in value) {\\n    if (value.hasOwnProperty(key)) {\\n      copy[key] = deepClone(value[key], visited);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return copy;\\n}"],[1137,"The Event Loop is the mechanism that handles asynchronous operations in Node.js. setImmediate is carried out after the input-output operations and the timers but before the next iteration of the event loop. It lets you postpone the execution of code until the current cycle has finished.","The Event Loop is the core of the asynchronous handling in Node.js. It manages the execution of code, handling the events and the callbacks in a particular order.\\nThe phases of the Event Loop:\\n\\n- Timers: The callbacks from `setTimeout` and `setInterval` are carried out\\n\\n- Pending callbacks: Postponed system operations are carried out (network errors, for example)\\n\\n- Poll: It handles new I/O events\\n\\n- Check: The `setImmediate` callbacks are carried out\\n\\n- Close callbacks: The closing callbacks are carried out (`socket.on(\'close\')`, for example)\\n\\nWhen setImmediate is carried out:\\n\\n- After the Poll phase and before the Close callbacks phase\\n\\n- In the current iteration of the event loop, but after the I/O operations\\n\\n- It lets you carry code out right after the current operations have finished\\n\\nConclusion:","console.log(\'Start\');\\n\\nsetImmediate(() => {\\n  console.log(\'setImmediate callback\');\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'setTimeout callback\');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(\'End\');\\n\\nStart\\nEnd\\nsetTimeout callback\\nsetImmediate callback"],[1138,"Blocking operations stop the execution of the code until the operation has finished, which can freeze an application. Non-blocking operations use callbacks or promises and let an application carry on working while it waits for the result.","Blocking operations are synchronous operations, which stop the execution of the main thread until they have finished.\\nExamples of blocking operations:\\n\\n- `fs.readFileSync()` — the synchronous reading of a file\\n\\n- `crypto.pbkdf2Sync()` — synchronous hashing\\n\\n- Lengthy computations in the main thread\\n\\nNon-blocking operations are asynchronous operations, which use callbacks, promises or async/await.\\nThe advantages of non-blocking operations:\\n\\n- They do not block the event loop\\n\\n- They let other requests be handled\\n\\n- They improve an application\'s scalability\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Blocking: for simple scripts or for initialisation\\n\\n- Non-blocking: for web servers and highly loaded applications","// A blocking operation\\nconst data = fs.readFileSync(\'file.txt\');\\nconsole.log(data);\\n\\n// A non-blocking operation\\nfs.readFile(\'file.txt\', (err, data) => {\\n  if (err) throw err;\\n  console.log(data);\\n});"],[1139,"Microservices offer independent deployment, scalability and technological variety, but they increase the complexity of the coordination, require a powerful infrastructure and can create problems with the consistency of the data.","A microservice architecture presupposes the splitting of an application into small, independent services, each of which is responsible for a particular business capability.\\nThe pluses of microservices:\\n\\n- Independent deployment — every service can be updated separately\\n\\n- Scalability — only the services you need can be scaled\\n\\n- Technological variety — different services can be written in different languages and technologies\\n\\n- Resilience to failures — the fall of one service does not always lead to the fall of the whole system\\n\\n- The separation of responsibility — different teams can work on different services\\n\\nThe minuses of microservices:\\n\\n- The complexity of the coordination — a multitude of services has to be managed\\n\\n- The complexity of debugging — the tracing of requests through several services\\n\\n- The overheads — an infrastructure for orchestration and monitoring is required\\n\\n- The complexity with the data — distributed transactions and eventual consistency\\n\\n- Network delays — the communication between the services goes over the network\\n\\nWhen to use them:\\n\\n- For large and complex applications\\n\\n- When flexibility and the frequent updating of components are needed\\n\\n- When there are several development teams",null],[1140,"For the communication of microservices, synchronous (REST, gRPC) and asynchronous (Kafka, RabbitMQ, NATS) transports are used. The synchronous ones require an immediate response; the asynchronous ones use message brokers to decouple the services.","The communication between microservices is a critical aspect of distributed systems. There are two main approaches:\\nSynchronous communication:\\n\\n- The client waits for an immediate response from the service\\n\\n- REST/HTTP — simple, human-readable, based on HTTP\\n\\n- gRPC — high-performance, binary, based on HTTP/2\\n\\n- It is suitable for request-response scenarios\\n\\nAsynchronous communication:\\n\\n- The services exchange messages through a broker\\n\\n- Kafka — high-performance, with persistence and replayability\\n\\n- RabbitMQ — AMQP, flexible routing, acknowledged delivery\\n\\n- NATS — lightweight, high-throughput, persistence optional\\n\\n- It is suitable for event-driven architectures\\n\\nExamples of the use:\\n\\nThe choice of the transport depends on:\\n\\n- The requirements of performance\\n\\n- The need for guaranteed delivery\\n\\n- The complexity of the implementation\\n\\n- The requirements of scalability","// A synchronous REST call\\naxios.get(\'http://user-service/users/123\')\\n\\n// The asynchronous sending of a message\\nkafkaProducer.send({\\n    topic: \'order-created\',\\n    messages: [{ value: JSON.stringify(order) }]\\n})"],[1141,"The main kinds of test: unit (the checking of individual components), integration (the checking of the interaction of components) and e2e (the checking of the whole system). They differ in their scope, their speed of execution and the complexity of their setup.","Testing is a critical part of the development of software, which ensures the quality and the reliability of the code.\\nThe main kinds of test:\\n\\n- Unit tests:\\n\\n- They check individual functions and modules\\n\\n- Fast, isolated\\n\\n- An example: a test of the function calculatePrice()\\n\\n- Integration tests:\\n\\n- They check the interaction of components\\n\\n- They test APIs, databases and external services\\n\\n- An example: a test of the creation of a user through the API\\n\\n- End-to-end (E2E) tests:\\n\\n- They check the whole system from beginning to end\\n\\n- They imitate the user\'s behaviour\\n\\n- Slow, difficult to maintain\\n\\n- An example: a test of the process of placing an order\\n\\n- Load tests:\\n\\n- They check the performance under load\\n\\n- They measure the response time and the throughput\\n\\nThe testing pyramid:\\n\\nThe recommendations:\\n\\n- Many unit tests (fast, reliable)\\n\\n- A moderate quantity of integration tests\\n\\n- A few E2E tests for the critical paths","      E2E tests\\n     /         \\\\\\n  Integration tests\\n /                 \\\\\\n   Unit tests (the base)"],[1142,"Optional chaining (`?.`) is an operator in JavaScript that lets you safely turn to the properties of nested objects without checking the existence of every level. If some property in the chain is equal to `null` or `undefined`, the expression returns `undefined` instead of an error. This simplifies code and makes it more readable.","Optional chaining is a syntax that helps you avoid errors when turning to the properties of objects that may be `null` or `undefined`.\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- The `?.` operator is used\\n\\n- If the property before the `?.` is equal to `null` or `undefined`, the expression returns `undefined`\\n\\n- If the property exists, the access takes place as usual\\n\\nExamples of the use:\\n\\nThe application:\\n\\n- For safe access to the properties of API responses\\n\\n- When working with configuration objects\\n\\n- For the simplification of code with multiple checks","const user = {\\n  profile: {\\n    name: \\"Alice\\",\\n    address: {\\n      city: \\"Moscow\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Without optional chaining (cumbersome)\\nconst city = user && user.profile && user.profile.address && user.profile.address.city;\\n\\n// With optional chaining (simple)\\nconst citySafe = user?.profile?.address?.city;\\nconsole.log(citySafe); // \\"Moscow\\"\\n\\n// If the property is absent\\nconst zipCode = user?.profile?.address?.zipCode;\\nconsole.log(zipCode); // undefined"],[1143,"`Pick`, `Omit` and `Partial` are TypeScript utility types for working with object types. `Pick` creates a type from the chosen properties of the original type. `Omit` creates a type excluding the properties specified. `Partial` makes all of a type\'s properties optional.","TypeScript provides several useful utility types for the manipulation of object types.\\nPick:\\nIt creates a new type, choosing only the properties specified from the original type.\\n\\nOmit:\\nIt creates a new type, excluding the properties specified.\\n\\nPartial:\\nIt makes all of a type\'s properties optional.\\n\\nThe practical application:\\n\\n- `Pick` for the creation of view types (DTOs)\\n\\n- `Omit` for the exclusion of sensitive data\\n\\n- `Partial` for update types (patch operations)","interface User {\\n  id: number;\\n  name: string;\\n  email: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\ntype UserPreview = Pick<User, \'id\' | \'name\'>;\\n// Equivalent to: { id: number; name: string; }\\n\\ntype UserWithoutEmail = Omit<User, \'email\'>;\\n// Equivalent to: { id: number; name: string; age: number; }\\n\\ntype PartialUser = Partial<User>;\\n// Equivalent to: { id?: number; name?: string; email?: string; age?: number; }"],[1144,"In Node.js the `fs` module is used for work with files. It provides synchronous and asynchronous methods. The asynchronous methods are preferable, since they do not block the event loop. For modern applications it is recommended that you use the Promise-based API (`fs.promises`).","Node.js provides the powerful `fs` (File System) module for work with files.\\nThe main approaches:\\n\\n- The synchronous methods (blocking):\\n\\n- The asynchronous methods with a callback:\\n\\n- The Promise-based API (recommended):\\n\\nThe main operations:\\n\\n- The reading of files (`readFile`)\\n\\n- The writing of files (`writeFile`)\\n\\n- The deletion of files (`unlink`)\\n\\n- Work with directories (`mkdir`, `readdir`)\\n\\nThe best practices:\\n\\n- Use the asynchronous methods in order to avoid blocking\\n\\n- Handle the errors by means of try-catch for the promise-based API\\n\\n- Use streams for work with large files","const fs = require(\'fs\');\\nconst data = fs.readFileSync(\'file.txt\', \'utf8\');\\n\\nfs.readFile(\'file.txt\', \'utf8\', (err, data) => {\\n  if (err) throw err;\\n  console.log(data);\\n});\\n\\nconst fs = require(\'fs\').promises;\\n\\nasync function readFile() {\\n  try {\\n    const data = await fs.readFile(\'file.txt\', \'utf8\');\\n    console.log(data);\\n  } catch (error) {\\n    console.error(error);\\n  }\\n}"],[1145,"CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a security mechanism of browsers, which permits or forbids requests to resources on another domain. It prevents cross-site requests that may be dangerous. The server has to send special headers in order to permit cross-domain requests.","CORS is an important mechanism of web security, which controls access to resources between different domains.\\nHow CORS works:\\n\\n- The browser sends a preliminary (preflight) OPTIONS request to check the permissions\\n\\n- The server answers with headers specifying the origins, methods and headers permitted\\n\\n- If the request is permitted, the browser sends the main request\\n\\nThe main CORS headers:\\n\\n- `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`: the domains permitted\\n\\n- `Access-Control-Allow-Methods`: the HTTP methods permitted\\n\\n- `Access-Control-Allow-Headers`: the headers permitted\\n\\nAn example of the configuration in Express.js:\\n\\nWhen a CORS error arises:\\n\\n- On requests between different domains\\n\\n- On the use of non-standard headers\\n\\n- On requests with credentials (cookies, authorisation)","const express = require(\'express\');\\nconst cors = require(\'cors\');\\n\\nconst app = express();\\n\\n// The simple configuration\\napp.use(cors());\\n\\n// The custom configuration\\napp.use(cors({\\n  origin: \'https://example.com\',\\n  methods: [\'GET\', \'POST\'],\\n  allowedHeaders: [\'Content-Type\']\\n}));"],[1146,"A JWT (JSON Web Token) is a standard for the secure transfer of information between parties in the form of a JSON object. The token consists of three parts: the header, the payload and the signature. These parts are separated by dots and encoded in Base64.","A JWT is a popular standard for authentication and authorisation in web applications.\\nThe structure of a JWT:\\nThe token has the form `header.payload.signature`\\n\\n- The Header:\\n\\n- It contains information about the type of the token and the algorithm of the signature\\n\\n- An example: `{\\"alg\\": \\"HS256\\", \\"typ\\": \\"JWT\\"}`\\n\\n- The Payload:\\n\\n- It contains the claims — information about the user and additional data\\n\\n- An example: `{\\"sub\\": \\"123\\", \\"name\\": \\"John\\", \\"exp\\": 1516239022}`\\n\\n- The Signature:\\n\\n- It is created by the signing of the encoded header and payload using a secret key\\n\\n- It guarantees the token\'s integrity\\n\\nAn example of the creation of a JWT in Node.js:\\n\\nThe advantages of a JWT:\\n\\n- Statelessness (it requires no storage on the server)\\n\\n- Support for different algorithms of signature\\n\\n- Easy verification of authenticity\\n\\nSecurity:\\n\\n- Always use HTTPS\\n\\n- Hold the secret keys securely\\n\\n- Set a sensible expiration time","const jwt = require(\'jsonwebtoken\');\\n\\nconst token = jwt.sign(\\n  { userId: 123, role: \'admin\' },\\n  \'secret-key\',\\n  { expiresIn: \'1h\' }\\n);\\nconsole.log(token); // header.payload.signature"],[1147,"MutationObserver callbacks are microtasks. They are carried out in the same phase of the event loop as a Promise, but after them. This ensures a high priority of execution and guarantees that observations of changes in the DOM are handled before the browser\'s next rendering.","MutationObserver uses microtasks in order to ensure the timely and efficient tracking of changes in the DOM.\\nThe place in the event loop:\\nThe order of the execution of the tasks:\\n\\n- Macrotasks: setTimeout, setInterval, I/O\\n\\n- Microtasks: Promise, queueMicrotask, MutationObserver\\n\\n- The rendering: the browser\'s repaint/reflow\\n\\n- The next iteration: the move to the next macrotask\\n\\nThe details of the implementation:\\nThe behaviour of MutationObserver:\\n\\nThe advantages of microtasks:\\nThe timeliness of the execution:\\n\\n- The handling of changes in the DOM before the next rendering\\n\\n- A guaranteed order relative to the other microtasks\\n\\n- A high priority in the event loop\\n\\nThe efficiency:\\n\\n- The grouping of multiple changes into a single callback\\n\\n- The avoidance of \\"jitter\\" on frequent changes\\n\\n- The optimisation of performance\\n\\nA comparison with the other approaches:\\nMutationObserver vs setTimeout:\\n\\nThe practical significance:\\n\\n- MutationObserver is ideal for reactive systems\\n\\n- It guarantees that the data is current before the visual update\\n\\n- It is used in modern frameworks for tracking changes","// The creation of the observer\\nconst observer = new MutationObserver((mutations) => {\\n  console.log(\'The DOM has changed\'); // This is a microtask\\n});\\n\\n// The observation of the changes\\nobserver.observe(document.body, {\\n  childList: true,\\n  subtree: true\\n});\\n\\n// An example of the order of the execution\\nconsole.log(\'Start\');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  console.log(\'setTimeout\'); // A macrotask\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Promise\'); // A microtask\\n});\\n\\n// A change in the DOM\\ndocument.body.appendChild(document.createElement(\'div\'));\\n\\nconsole.log(\'End\');\\n\\n// The order of the output:\\n// \\"Start\\"\\n// \\"End\\" \\n// \\"Promise\\" - the Promise microtask\\n// \\"The DOM has changed\\" - the MutationObserver microtask\\n// \\"setTimeout\\" - the macrotask\\n\\n// MutationObserver (a microtask)\\nobserver.observe(element, { attributes: true });\\nelement.setAttribute(\'data-test\', \'value\');\\n// The callback will be carried out before the next rendering\\n\\n// setTimeout (a macrotask)\\nelement.setAttribute(\'data-test\', \'value\');\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n  // The callback will be carried out after the rendering\\n}, 0);"],[1148,"For the caching of data, use LocalStorage for small volumes of textual data (up to 5 MB) and IndexedDB for large volumes and complex structures. If IndexedDB is not supported, you can use LocalStorage as a fallback, or implement a polyfill for IndexedDB. For really old browsers you can use cookies or server-side storage.","The choice of the storage technology depends on the volume of the data, its structure and the requirements of performance.\\nA comparison of the storage technologies:\\nLocalStorage:\\n\\n- The volume: ~5 MB\\n\\n- The data type: strings only\\n\\n- The access: synchronous\\n\\n- The use:\\n\\nIndexedDB:\\n\\n- The volume: ~50 % of the free space\\n\\n- The data type: any JS objects\\n\\n- The access: asynchronous\\n\\n- The use:\\n\\nThe fallback strategy:\\nThe determination of the support:\\n\\nA universal adapter:\\n\\nPolyfills:\\n\\n- The use of libraries such as `localForage`\\n\\n- A custom implementation on LocalStorage for small volumes of data\\n\\n- Server-side caching as the last resort","// Simple data\\nlocalStorage.setItem(\'user\', JSON.stringify(userData));\\nconst user = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\'user\'));\\n\\nconst request = indexedDB.open(\'MyDatabase\', 1);\\nrequest.onsuccess = (event) => {\\n  const db = event.target.result;\\n  const transaction = db.transaction([\'store\'], \'readwrite\');\\n  const store = transaction.objectStore(\'store\');\\n  store.put(data, \'key\');\\n};\\n\\nconst isIndexedDBSupported = () => {\\n  return \'indexedDB\' in window;\\n};\\n\\nconst isLocalStorageSupported = () => {\\n  try {\\n    localStorage.setItem(\'test\', \'test\');\\n    localStorage.removeItem(\'test\');\\n    return true;\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    return false;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nclass StorageAdapter {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.supported = this.detectSupport();\\n  }\\n\\n  detectSupport() {\\n    if (isIndexedDBSupported()) return \'indexeddb\';\\n    if (isLocalStorageSupported()) return \'localstorage\';\\n    return \'none\';\\n  }\\n\\n  async set(key, value) {\\n    switch (this.supported) {\\n      case \'indexeddb\':\\n        return await this.setIndexedDB(key, value);\\n      case \'localstorage\':\\n        return this.setLocalStorage(key, value);\\n      default:\\n        throw new Error(\'No storage support\');\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  setLocalStorage(key, value) {\\n    localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(value));\\n  }\\n\\n  async setIndexedDB(key, value) {\\n    // The IndexedDB implementation\\n  }\\n}"],[1149,"Hoisting is a mechanism in JavaScript by which the declarations of variables and functions are \\"lifted\\" to the beginning of their scope. The declarations of functions are lifted completely, while for variables declared through `var` only the declaration is lifted and not the initialisation. `let` and `const` are also hoisted, but they are not initialised until the actual declaration.","Hoisting affects the availability of variables and functions before their actual declaration in the code.\\nThe behaviour for the different types:\\nThe declarations of functions:\\n\\nvar variables:\\n\\nlet and const:\\n\\nThe order of the hoisting:\\n\\n- The declarations of functions\\n\\n- The declarations of variables\\n\\n- The assignments of values\\n\\nAn example with conflicts:\\n\\nThe practical consequences:\\n\\n- Always declare variables at the beginning of the scope\\n\\n- Use `let` and `const` instead of `var`\\n\\n- Avoid the overriding of functions by variables","// It works thanks to hoisting\\nconsole.log(sayHello()); // \\"Hello\\"\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n  return \\"Hello\\";\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(x); // undefined (the declaration has been hoisted)\\nvar x = 5;\\nconsole.log(x); // 5\\n\\n// Equivalent to:\\nvar x;\\nconsole.log(x); // undefined\\nx = 5;\\nconsole.log(x); // 5\\n\\nconsole.log(y); // ReferenceError\\nlet y = 10;\\n\\n// The temporal dead zone (TDZ)\\n// from the beginning of the block to the declaration\\n\\nvar myFunction = 1;\\nfunction myFunction() {\\n  return 2;\\n}\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof myFunction); // \\"number\\"\\n// The function was hoisted first, but was then overwritten by the variable"],[1150,"For the forcible refreshing of a page, use `location.reload()`, which reloads the current page. For a complete reload with the clearing of the cache, use `location.reload(true)`. You can also set `location.href = location.href` or use `location.replace()` to replace the current page.","There are several methods of refreshing a page, each with its own particulars.\\nThe methods of refreshing:\\n1. location.reload():\\n\\n2. The resetting of the URL:\\n\\n3. The navigation methods:\\n\\nThe differences between the methods:\\nlocation.reload() vs location.href:\\n\\n- `reload()` always makes a request to the server\\n\\n- `location.href` may take the page from the cache\\n\\n- `reload(true)` guarantees a fresh version\\n\\nAn example with handling:\\n\\nThe special cases:\\nRefreshing with parameters:\\n\\nAn SPA (Single Page Application):\\n\\nThe recommendations:\\n\\n- Use `location.reload()` for most cases\\n\\n- Add a confirmation for the user\\n\\n- In an SPA, prefer the refreshing of the data without a reload","// A simple refresh\\nlocation.reload();\\n\\n// A forcible refresh with the clearing of the cache\\nlocation.reload(true);\\n\\n// The effect resembles reload()\\nlocation.href = location.href;\\n\\n// Without the addition to the browser\'s history\\nlocation.replace(location.href);\\n\\n// The use of the history API\\nhistory.go(0);\\n\\n// Analogous to reload()\\nhistory.go();\\n\\nfunction forceReload() {\\n  try {\\n    // We try to refresh forcibly\\n    location.reload(true);\\n  } catch (e) {\\n    // A fallback for older browsers\\n    location.href = location.href + \'?force=\' + Date.now();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// A refresh with a confirmation\\nfunction confirmReload() {\\n  if (confirm(\'Refresh the page?\')) {\\n    location.reload();\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// The addition of a timestamp in order to avoid caching\\nlocation.search = \'?refresh=\' + Date.now();\\n\\n// In an SPA, instead of a complete refresh\\nwindow.dispatchEvent(new Event(\'forceRefresh\'));\\n// The components handle the event and refresh the data"],[1151,"Only Function Declarations are hoisted completely. Function Expressions, arrow functions included, are not hoisted — only the declaration of the variable is hoisted and not the assignment of the function. The declarations of classes are not hoisted either.","The behaviour of hoisting differs substantially depending on the way the function is declared.\\nA Function Declaration (complete hoisting):\\n\\nA Function Expression (partial hoisting):\\n\\nArrow functions:\\n\\nClasses:\\n\\nA practical example:\\n\\nThe recommendations:\\n\\n- Use Function Declarations for utility functions\\n\\n- For callbacks and methods, use Function Expressions\\n\\n- Always declare functions before their use","// It works - the function is completely available\\nsayHello(); // \\"Hello\\"\\n\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n  return \\"Hello\\";\\n}\\n\\n// It does not work - the variable has been hoisted, but it is equal to undefined\\nsayHello(); // TypeError: sayHello is not a function\\n\\nvar sayHello = function() {\\n  return \\"Hello\\";\\n};\\n\\n// It does not work - analogously to a Function Expression\\nsayHello(); // ReferenceError (let/const) or TypeError (var)\\n\\nconst sayHello = () => {\\n  return \\"Hello\\";\\n};\\n\\n// It does not work - classes are not hoisted\\nconst obj = new MyClass(); // ReferenceError\\n\\nclass MyClass {\\n  constructor() {}\\n}\\n\\n// A Function Declaration - it works\\nconsole.log(add(2, 3)); // 5\\nfunction add(a, b) { return a + b; }\\n\\n// A Function Expression - it does not work\\nconsole.log(multiply(2, 3)); // TypeError\\nvar multiply = function(a, b) { return a * b; };\\n\\n// An arrow function - it does not work  \\nconsole.log(divide(6, 2)); // ReferenceError\\nconst divide = (a, b) => a / b;"],[1152,"For the deep copying of objects, use `JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj))` for simple objects, `structuredClone()` for modern browsers, or libraries of the Lodash `_.cloneDeep()` kind. A manual recursive function is also suitable for complex cases. Every method has its own limitations and particulars.","Deep copying creates a completely independent copy of an object with all of its nested properties.\\nThe methods of copying:\\n1. The JSON method (simple, but with limitations):\\n\\n2. structuredClone (the modern standard):\\n\\n3. A recursive function:\\n\\n4. Lodash cloneDeep:\\n\\nAn example with circular references:\\n\\nThe recommendations:\\n\\n- Use `structuredClone` for modern applications\\n\\n- For legacy support, Lodash or the JSON method\\n\\n- For special cases, a custom recursive function","const obj = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };\\nconst copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj));\\n\\n// The limitations:\\n// - It ignores functions, undefined and Symbol\\n// - It converts a Date into a string\\n// - It loses the prototypes and the constructors\\n\\n// The native method for deep copying\\nconst obj = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };\\nconst copy = structuredClone(obj);\\n\\n// It supports:\\n// - Map, Set, Date, RegExp, ArrayBuffer\\n// - Circular references\\n\\nfunction deepClone(obj, hash = new WeakMap()) {\\n  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== \'object\') return obj;\\n  if (hash.has(obj)) return hash.get(obj);\\n  if (obj instanceof Date) return new Date(obj);\\n  if (obj instanceof RegExp) return new RegExp(obj);\\n\\n  const clone = Array.isArray(obj) ? [] : {};\\n  hash.set(obj, clone);\\n\\n  for (let key in obj) {\\n    if (obj.hasOwnProperty(key)) {\\n      clone[key] = deepClone(obj[key], hash);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  return clone;\\n}\\n\\nconst _ = require(\'lodash\');\\nconst copy = _.cloneDeep(original);\\n\\nconst obj = { a: 1 };\\nobj.self = obj;\\n\\n// The JSON method will fall over\\n// JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)); // TypeError\\n\\n// structuredClone works\\nconst copy = structuredClone(obj); // Successfully"],[1153,"The spread operator \\"unfolds\\" the elements of arrays or the properties of objects, while rest parameters \\"gather\\" the remaining arguments into an array or the remaining properties into an object. Spread is used on the right-hand side of an expression and rest on the left, in the context of destructuring or of a function\'s parameters.","One and the same `...` operator is used for two opposite operations depending on the context.\\nThe spread operator (the unfolding):\\nFor arrays:\\n\\nFor objects:\\n\\nRest parameters (the gathering):\\nIn a function\'s parameters:\\n\\nIn the destructuring of arrays:\\n\\nIn the destructuring of objects:\\n\\nPractical examples:\\nThe combination:\\n\\nCloning with an exclusion:","const arr1 = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst arr2 = [4, 5, 6];\\nconst combined = [...arr1, ...arr2]; // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]\\n\\n// The copying of an array\\nconst copy = [...arr1]; // [1, 2, 3]\\n\\nconst obj1 = { a: 1, b: 2 };\\nconst obj2 = { c: 3, d: 4 };\\nconst merged = { ...obj1, ...obj2 }; // { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3, d: 4 }\\n\\n// The overriding of properties\\nconst updated = { ...obj1, b: 20 }; // { a: 1, b: 20 }\\n\\nfunction sum(...numbers) {\\n  return numbers.reduce((total, num) => total + num, 0);\\n}\\nsum(1, 2, 3, 4); // 10\\n\\nconst [first, second, ...rest] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\n// first = 1, second = 2, rest = [3, 4, 5]\\n\\nconst { a, b, ...other } = { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3, d: 4 };\\n// a = 1, b = 2, other = { c: 3, d: 4 }\\n\\nfunction process(first, second, ...others) {\\n  console.log(first, second); // 1, 2\\n  console.log(others); // [3, 4, 5]\\n}\\n\\nconst numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\\nprocess(...numbers); // Spread for the passing, rest for the gathering\\n\\nconst original = { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3, d: 4 };\\nconst { b, ...withoutB } = original;\\n// withoutB = { a: 1, c: 3, d: 4 }"],[1154,"Promises came to replace callback hell and provided a cleaner way of working with asynchronous operations. A promise has three states: pending, fulfilled (carried out successfully) and rejected (carried out with an error). Once it has passed into the fulfilled or the rejected state, a promise changes no more.","Promises solve the problems of asynchronous code that were characteristic of the callback-based approach.\\nThe evolution of asynchronicity:\\nCallback Hell:\\n\\nThe Promise solution:\\n\\nThe states of a Promise:\\n1. Pending:\\n\\n2. Fulfilled:\\n\\n3. Rejected:\\n\\nThe scheme of the transitions:\\n\\nThe determination of the state:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The state changes only once\\n\\n- The execution of a Promise cannot be cancelled\\n\\n- A chain of then/catch creates new Promises\\n\\nThe advantages over callbacks:\\n\\n- Readable sequential code\\n\\n- The centralised handling of errors\\n\\n- The ability to combine them through Promise.all/race","// The problem of nested callbacks\\ngetData(function(data1) {\\n  processData(data1, function(data2) {\\n    saveData(data2, function(data3) {\\n      displayData(data3, function() {\\n        // And so on...\\n      });\\n    });\\n  });\\n});\\n\\ngetData()\\n  .then(processData)\\n  .then(saveData)\\n  .then(displayData)\\n  .catch(handleError);\\n\\nconst promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  // The asynchronous operation has not yet finished\\n});\\n// promise.state = \'pending\'\\n\\nconst promise = Promise.resolve(\'success\');\\n// or\\nconst promise = new Promise((resolve) => {\\n  resolve(\'success\');\\n});\\n// promise.state = \'fulfilled\'\\n\\nconst promise = Promise.reject(new Error(\'failure\'));\\n// or\\nconst promise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n  reject(new Error(\'failure\'));\\n});\\n// promise.state = \'rejected\'\\n\\nPending\\n   ↓\\nFulfilled (with a value)\\n   or  \\nRejected (with the reason for the error)\\n\\nconst promise = someAsyncOperation();\\n\\npromise.then(\\n  value => {\\n    console.log(\'Fulfilled:\', value);\\n  },\\n  error => {\\n    console.log(\'Rejected:\', error);\\n  }\\n);"],[1155,"The main static methods of Promise: `Promise.all()` for waiting for every promise, `Promise.race()` for the first one to finish, `Promise.allSettled()` for every result (successful and unsuccessful), `Promise.any()` for the first successful one, and `Promise.resolve()` and `Promise.reject()` for the creation of promises that are already resolved or rejected.","The static methods of Promise provide powerful tools for working with a multitude of asynchronous operations.\\nThe methods for collections:\\nPromise.all() — every one successful:\\n\\nPromise.race() — the first to finish:\\n\\nPromise.allSettled() — every result:\\n\\nPromise.any() — the first successful one:\\n\\nThe methods of creation:\\nPromise.resolve() — a successful promise:\\n\\nPromise.reject() — a promise with an error:","const promises = [\\n  fetch(\'/api/users\'),\\n  fetch(\'/api/products\'),\\n  fetch(\'/api/orders\')\\n];\\n\\nPromise.all(promises)\\n  .then(([users, products, orders]) => {\\n    // Every promise has been carried out successfully\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    // At least one promise has been rejected\\n  });\\n\\nconst timeout = new Promise((_, reject) => {\\n  setTimeout(() => reject(new Error(\'Timeout\')), 5000);\\n});\\n\\nPromise.race([fetch(\'/api/data\'), timeout])\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // The fetch finished before the timeout\\n  })\\n  .catch(error => {\\n    // The timeout fired first\\n  });\\n\\nconst promises = [promise1, promise2, promise3];\\n\\nPromise.allSettled(promises)\\n  .then(results => {\\n    results.forEach(result => {\\n      if (result.status === \'fulfilled\') {\\n        console.log(\'Success:\', result.value);\\n      } else {\\n        console.log(\'Error:\', result.reason);\\n      }\\n    });\\n  });\\n\\nconst mirrors = [\\n  fetch(\'https://mirror1.com/data\'),\\n  fetch(\'https://mirror2.com/data\'),\\n  fetch(\'https://mirror3.com/data\')\\n];\\n\\nPromise.any(mirrors)\\n  .then(data => {\\n    // The first successful response\\n  })\\n  .catch(errors => {\\n    // Every promise has been rejected\\n  });\\n\\nconst resolved = Promise.resolve(42);\\n// Equivalent to: new Promise(resolve => resolve(42))\\n\\n// The use for caching\\nconst cache = new Map();\\nfunction getData(key) {\\n  if (cache.has(key)) {\\n    return Promise.resolve(cache.get(key));\\n  }\\n  return fetchData(key);\\n}\\n\\nconst rejected = Promise.reject(new Error(\'Failure\'));\\n// Equivalent to: new Promise((_, reject) => reject(error))"],[1156,"For work with WebSockets you can use the Socket.IO library, which provides additional capabilities on top of the native WebSocket API, such as automatic reconnection, room management and fallback polling. Also popular are ws for a Node.js server, uWebSockets.js for high performance, and the native WebSocket API for simple cases.","Various libraries provide different levels of abstraction for work with WebSockets.\\nSocket.IO (the most popular):\\nThe client side:\\n\\nThe server side (Node.js):\\n\\nThe alternative libraries:\\nws (lightweight, for Node.js):\\n\\nuWebSockets.js (high performance):\\n\\nThe native WebSocket API:\\n\\nThe additional capabilities of Socket.IO:\\nRooms:","import io from \'socket.io-client\';\\n\\nconst socket = io(\'http://localhost:3000\', {\\n  transports: [\'websocket\', \'polling\'] // Fallback\\n});\\n\\n// The events of the connection\\nsocket.on(\'connect\', () => {\\n  console.log(\'Connected to server\');\\n});\\n\\n// The sending of data\\nsocket.emit(\'chat message\', \'Hello world\');\\n\\n// The receiving of data\\nsocket.on(\'chat message\', (msg) => {\\n  console.log(\'Message received:\', msg);\\n});\\n\\n// Automatic reconnection\\nsocket.on(\'reconnect\', (attempt) => {\\n  console.log(`Reconnected after ${attempt} attempts`);\\n});\\n\\nconst express = require(\'express\');\\nconst http = require(\'http\');\\nconst { Server } = require(\'socket.io\');\\n\\nconst app = express();\\nconst server = http.createServer(app);\\nconst io = new Server(server);\\n\\nio.on(\'connection\', (socket) => {\\n  console.log(\'User connected:\', socket.id);\\n\\n  socket.on(\'chat message\', (msg) => {\\n    // A broadcast to every client\\n    io.emit(\'chat message\', msg);\\n  });\\n\\n  socket.on(\'disconnect\', () => {\\n    console.log(\'User disconnected\');\\n  });\\n});\\n\\nserver.listen(3000);\\n\\nconst WebSocket = require(\'ws\');\\nconst wss = new WebSocket.Server({ port: 8080 });\\n\\nwss.on(\'connection\', (ws) => {\\n  ws.on(\'message\', (message) => {\\n    console.log(\'Received:\', message);\\n  });\\n\\n  ws.send(\'Hello from server\');\\n});\\n\\nconst uWS = require(\'uWebSockets.js\');\\n\\nuWS.App().ws(\'/*\', {\\n  open: (ws) => {\\n    console.log(\'Client connected\');\\n  },\\n  message: (ws, message) => {\\n    ws.send(message);\\n  }\\n}).listen(3000);\\n\\nconst socket = new WebSocket(\'ws://localhost:8080\');\\n\\nsocket.onopen = () => {\\n  socket.send(\'Hello Server!\');\\n};\\n\\nsocket.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'Message from server:\', event.data);\\n};\\n\\nsocket.onclose = () => {\\n  console.log(\'Connection closed\');\\n};\\n\\n// The server\\nsocket.join(\'room1\');\\nio.to(\'room1\').emit(\'message\', \'Hello room\');\\n\\n// Namespaces\\nconst adminNamespace = io.of(\'/admin\');\\nadminNamespace.on(\'connection\', (socket) => {\\n  // For the admins only\\n});"],[1157,"If the server does not support WebSockets, you can use Server-Sent Events (SSE) for one-way communication, long polling to emulate real time, short polling for periodic requests, or WebRTC for peer-to-peer connections. You can also use commercial services such as Pusher or Firebase for real-time functionality.","There are several strategies for emulating real-time behaviour without WebSockets.\\n1. Server-Sent Events (SSE):\\nThe client:\\n\\nThe server (Node.js + Express):\\n\\n2. Long Polling:\\nThe client:\\n\\nThe server:\\n\\n3. Short Polling:\\nThe client:\\n\\n4. Comet (a hidden iframe):\\n\\n5. WebRTC Data Channels:\\n\\nThe fallback strategy:","const eventSource = new EventSource(\'/updates\');\\n\\neventSource.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);\\n  console.log(\'Update received:\', data);\\n};\\n\\neventSource.onerror = (error) => {\\n  console.error(\'SSE error:\', error);\\n};\\n\\n// The closing of the connection\\n// eventSource.close();\\n\\napp.get(\'/updates\', (req, res) => {\\n  res.writeHead(200, {\\n    \'Content-Type\': \'text/event-stream\',\\n    \'Cache-Control\': \'no-cache\',\\n    \'Connection\': \'keep-alive\',\\n  });\\n\\n  // The sending of data every 5 seconds\\n  const interval = setInterval(() => {\\n    res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify({ time: Date.now() })}\\\\n\\\\n`);\\n  }, 5000);\\n\\n  // The cleanup when the connection breaks\\n  req.on(\'close\', () => {\\n    clearInterval(interval);\\n  });\\n});\\n\\nfunction longPoll() {\\n  fetch(\'/messages\')\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(messages => {\\n      // The handling of the messages\\n      processMessages(messages);\\n      \\n      // We begin the next request immediately\\n      longPoll();\\n    })\\n    .catch(error => {\\n      console.error(\'Polling error:\', error);\\n      // A repeat in 5 seconds on an error\\n      setTimeout(longPoll, 5000);\\n    });\\n}\\n\\n// The start of the long polling\\nlongPoll();\\n\\nconst messagesQueue = [];\\n\\napp.get(\'/messages\', (req, res) => {\\n  // We wait for new messages for up to 30 seconds\\n  const checkMessages = () => {\\n    if (messagesQueue.length > 0) {\\n      res.json(messagesQueue.splice(0));\\n    } else {\\n      setTimeout(checkMessages, 1000);\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  // A timeout of 30 seconds\\n  const timeout = setTimeout(() => {\\n    res.json([]);\\n  }, 30000);\\n\\n  checkMessages();\\n});\\n\\n// The addition of a message\\napp.post(\'/messages\', (req, res) => {\\n  messagesQueue.push(req.body);\\n  res.json({ status: \'ok\' });\\n});\\n\\n// Periodic requests every 5 seconds\\nsetInterval(() => {\\n  fetch(\'/updates\')\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(updates => {\\n      if (updates.length > 0) {\\n        handleUpdates(updates);\\n      }\\n    });\\n}, 5000);\\n\\n// The historical approach\\nfunction createCometConnection() {\\n  const iframe = document.createElement(\'iframe\');\\n  iframe.style.display = \'none\';\\n  iframe.src = \'/comet-endpoint\';\\n  document.body.appendChild(iframe);\\n  \\n  // The server gradually sends data into the iframe\\n}\\n\\n// A peer-to-peer connection\\nconst peerConnection = new RTCPeerConnection();\\nconst dataChannel = peerConnection.createDataChannel(\'chat\');\\n\\ndataChannel.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'Received:\', event.data);\\n};\\n\\ndataChannel.onopen = () => {\\n  dataChannel.send(\'Hello via WebRTC!\');\\n};\\n\\nfunction createRealtimeConnection(url) {\\n  // We check the support for WebSockets\\n  if (\'WebSocket\' in window) {\\n    try {\\n      return new WebSocket(url);\\n    } catch (e) {\\n      // WebSocket is not available, we use a fallback\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  // We check the support for SSE\\n  if (\'EventSource\' in window) {\\n    return new EventSource(url);\\n  }\\n  \\n  // We use long polling as the last option\\n  return createLongPollingConnection(url);\\n}"],[1158,"To move the execution of a script out into a separate thread you can use Web Workers for parallel execution, Service Workers for background tasks, or Worker Threads in Node.js. You can also use `setTimeout` and `setImmediate` to split heavy tasks into parts, or `requestIdleCallback` for execution during the browser\'s idle time.","Various APIs let you carry code out outside the main thread in order to improve performance.\\n1. Web Workers:\\nThe creation of a worker:\\n\\nworker.js:\\n\\n2. Service Workers:\\nThe registration:\\n\\n3. Worker Threads in Node.js:\\n\\n4. The splitting of heavy tasks:\\nThe use of setTimeout:\\n\\nThe use of requestIdleCallback:\\n\\n5. OffscreenCanvas for graphics:\\n\\nThe limitations of Workers:\\n\\n- There is no access to the DOM\\n\\n- Limited access to the window API\\n\\n- The data is passed through messages (serialized)\\n\\n- Debugging is more complex","// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');\\n\\n// The sending of data into the worker\\nworker.postMessage({ type: \'process\', data: largeArray });\\n\\n// The receiving of the results\\nworker.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  console.log(\'Result from worker:\', event.data);\\n};\\n\\n// The handling of errors\\nworker.onerror = (error) => {\\n  console.error(\'Worker error:\', error);\\n};\\n\\n// The handling of the messages from the main thread\\nself.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  const { type, data } = event.data;\\n  \\n  if (type === \'process\') {\\n    // The heavy computations\\n    const result = heavyComputation(data);\\n    \\n    // The sending of the result back\\n    self.postMessage(result);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfunction heavyComputation(data) {\\n  // The computations that do not block the main thread\\n  return data.map(item => item * 2);\\n}\\n\\n// The registration of the service worker\\nif (\'serviceWorker\' in navigator) {\\n  navigator.serviceWorker.register(\'/sw.js\')\\n    .then(registration => {\\n      console.log(\'Service Worker registered\');\\n    });\\n}\\n\\n// sw.js - the caching and the background tasks\\nself.addEventListener(\'fetch\', (event) => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request)\\n      .then(response => response || fetch(event.request))\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nconst { Worker } = require(\'worker_threads\');\\n\\nfunction runService(workerData) {\\n  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {\\n    const worker = new Worker(\'./worker.js\', { workerData });\\n    worker.on(\'message\', resolve);\\n    worker.on(\'error\', reject);\\n    worker.on(\'exit\', (code) => {\\n      if (code !== 0) {\\n        reject(new Error(`Worker stopped with exit code ${code}`));\\n      }\\n    });\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n// worker.js\\nconst { workerData, parentPort } = require(\'worker_threads\');\\nparentPort.postMessage(heavyComputation(workerData));\\n\\nfunction processInChunks(array, chunkSize, callback) {\\n  let index = 0;\\n\\n  function processChunk() {\\n    const chunk = array.slice(index, index + chunkSize);\\n    \\n    // The handling of the chunk\\n    chunk.forEach(processItem);\\n    \\n    index += chunkSize;\\n    \\n    if (index < array.length) {\\n      // We continue in the next frame\\n      setTimeout(processChunk, 0);\\n    } else {\\n      callback();\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  processChunk();\\n}\\n\\nfunction processDuringIdleTime(tasks) {\\n  function processTask(deadline) {\\n    while (tasks.length > 0 && deadline.timeRemaining() > 0) {\\n      const task = tasks.shift();\\n      executeTask(task);\\n    }\\n\\n    if (tasks.length > 0) {\\n      requestIdleCallback(processTask);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  requestIdleCallback(processTask);\\n}\\n\\n// The rendering of a canvas in a worker\\nconst offscreen = canvas.transferControlToOffscreen();\\nconst worker = new Worker(\'canvas-worker.js\');\\n\\nworker.postMessage({ canvas: offscreen }, [offscreen]);\\n\\n// canvas-worker.js\\nself.onmessage = function(event) {\\n  const canvas = event.data.canvas;\\n  const ctx = canvas.getContext(\'2d\');\\n  \\n  // The rendering without the blocking of the main thread\\n  ctx.fillStyle = \'red\';\\n  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, canvas.width, canvas.height);\\n};"],[1159,"A monolith is a single application in which every component is deployed together. Microservices are a set of small independent services. A monolith is simpler to develop and deploy at the start, but it is harder to scale and to change. Microservices let you scale and develop parts of the system independently, but they add complexity in the orchestration and the monitoring.","The choice between a monolith and microservices is a compromise between simplicity and flexibility.\\nThe monolithic architecture:\\n\\n- The pluses:\\n\\n- Simplicity of development: A single code base, simple communication between the components.\\n\\n- Simplicity of deployment: One application is deployed.\\n\\n- Simplicity of debugging: Every log and trace is in one place.\\n\\n- The minuses:\\n\\n- The difficulty of scaling: Only the whole application can be scaled.\\n\\n- Technological debt: Over time the code base becomes cumbersome and difficult to comprehend.\\n\\n- Reliability: The fall of one component can lead to the fall of the whole system.\\n\\nThe microservice architecture:\\n\\n- The pluses:\\n\\n- Independent scaling: Every service can be scaled separately.\\n\\n- Flexibility of the technologies: For every service a suitable technology stack can be used.\\n\\n- Resilience to failures: The fall of one service does not always lead to the stopping of the whole system.\\n\\n- Independent teams: Teams can work on different services independently.\\n\\n- The minuses:\\n\\n- High complexity: The complexity of the network interactions, the distributed transactions, the monitoring and the debugging.\\n\\n- The overheads: It requires an infrastructure for orchestration (Kubernetes), the discovery of services and logging.\\n\\n- The complexity of deployment: The deployment of a multitude of services has to be coordinated.\\n\\nWhen to use which:\\n\\n- Use a monolith: for small projects, for start-ups, for teams with limited resources, or when the requirements of the product have not yet stabilised.\\n\\n- Consider microservices: for large, complex systems with clear domain boundaries, when the independent scaling of the components is required and there is a team ready for the operational complexity.\\n\\nConclusion: It is not worth starting with microservices \\"for the future\\". Begin with a monolith, and when weighty reasons appear (bottlenecks in the scaling, for example), single services out.",null],[1160,"SOLID is a set of five principles that help you write comprehensible, flexible and easily maintainable code. They include: Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation and Dependency Inversion.","These principles are aimed at the creation of software that is easy to comprehend, to extend and to modify.\\nThe decoding of the SOLID principles:\\n\\n- S: The Single Responsibility Principle\\n\\n- The definition: Every class or module should have one and only one reason to change, that is, one responsibility.\\n\\n- An example: The `UserService` class should be responsible only for the business logic of the users and not for the sending of email or for logging to a file. Those tasks have to be moved out into separate classes (`EmailService`, `Logger`).\\n\\n- O: The Open/Closed Principle\\n\\n- The definition: Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification.\\n\\n- An example: Instead of changing an existing function in order to add a new type, it is better to use polymorphism. A new class is added, which implements a common interface, and the main logic works with that interface.\\n\\n- L: The Liskov Substitution Principle\\n\\n- The definition: The objects in a program should be replaceable with instances of their subtypes without a change in the program\'s correctness.\\n\\n- An example: If you have a function that takes the base class `Bird`, it should also work correctly with its descendant `Penguin` (which cannot fly), without breaking because of that.\\n\\n- I: The Interface Segregation Principle\\n\\n- The definition: Many specialised interfaces are better than one universal one. Clients should not depend on methods that they do not use.\\n\\n- An example: Instead of a large `IMultiFunctionDevice` interface with the methods `print()`, `scan()` and `fax()`, it is better to create separate interfaces `IPrinter` and `IScanner`. Then an old printer will be able to implement only `IPrinter`.\\n\\n- D: The Dependency Inversion Principle\\n\\n- The definition:\\n\\n- The modules of the upper levels should not depend on the modules of the lower levels. Both types of module should depend on abstractions.\\n\\n- Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.\\n\\n- An example: A service (`UserService`) should not directly create an instance of a repository for work with the database (`UserRepository`). Instead it should depend on an abstraction (the `IUserRepository` interface). The concrete implementation (`PostgresUserRepository`, for example) is injected from outside (through the constructor), often by means of a DI container, as in Nest.js.\\n\\nConclusion: Following the SOLID principles leads to the creation of code with low coupling and high cohesion, which eases its testing, refactoring and development in the long term.",null],[1161,"`setInterval` carries a function out with a fixed delay, taking no account of the screen\'s refresh rate, which can lead to skipped frames and superfluous computations. `requestAnimationFrame` synchronises the execution of the animation code with the screen\'s refresh rate (usually 60 frames per second), which makes the animation smooth and efficient. It also stops automatically when the user moves to another tab, saving resources. For smooth animations `requestAnimationFrame` should always be preferred.","Animation is the process of changing an element\'s properties over time. In JavaScript different timers can be used for this, but they differ greatly in their behaviour and their efficiency.\\nsetInterval\\n\\n- The principle of the working: It runs a function repeatedly after a given fixed interval of time (in milliseconds).\\n\\n- The problems:\\n\\n- It is not synchronised with the painting: It does not know when the browser is ready to repaint the page. That can lead to a \\"conflict\\" — the function tries to change a style while the browser is busy painting, because of which a frame is skipped and the animation jerks.\\n\\n- The working in the background: The timer carries on ticking even when the tab is inactive, wasting the battery\'s charge and computational resources in vain.\\n\\nrequestAnimationFrame (rAF)\\n\\n- The principle of the working: It tells the browser that you want to carry out an animation. The browser itself optimises the call of your function, carrying it out immediately before the page\'s next paint.\\n\\n- The advantages:\\n\\n- The synchronisation: It guarantees that your animation code is carried out at the ideal moment for the update of the frame, ensuring maximum smoothness.\\n\\n- The efficiency: The browser combines every `requestAnimationFrame` call in one frame, and it also suspends the animation on inactive tabs.\\n\\n- The precision: It passes a timestamp into the callback function, which lets you compute the animation precisely even if the frame rate fluctuates.\\n\\nConclusion: To create smooth, performant and resource-efficient animations, always use `requestAnimationFrame`. `setInterval` and `setTimeout` are suitable for tasks that do not require precise synchronisation with the painting (the periodic checking of state, for example).","// An example with setInterval (NOT RECOMMENDED for animations)\\nconst intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n  moveElementByOnePixel(); // We move the element\\n  if (animationIsComplete) clearInterval(intervalId);\\n}, 16); // ~60fps (1000ms / 60 ≈ 16ms)\\n\\n// An example with requestAnimationFrame (the RECOMMENDED way)\\nfunction animate(timestamp) {\\n  // We compute how far to shift the element on the basis of the timestamp\\n  moveElementBasedOnTime(timestamp);\\n\\n  if (animationIsComplete) return; // We stop the animation\\n\\n  requestAnimationFrame(animate); // We request the next frame\\n}\\n\\nrequestAnimationFrame(animate); // We start the animation"],[1162,"A pure function is a function that with the same input arguments always returns the same result and has no side effects (it does not change the external state or the arguments passed to it). Its working depends only on its input data, which makes its behaviour predictable and easily testable.","Pure functions are a cornerstone of functional programming and of the writing of reliable code.\\nThe characteristics of a pure function:\\n\\n- Determinism: For one and the same arguments it always returns one and the same result. It does not depend on hidden state or on changing external data (global variables or the current time, for example).\\n\\n- The absence of side effects: It produces no observable changes outside the function. This means that it:\\n\\n- Does not change its input arguments.\\n\\n- Does not modify global variables.\\n\\n- Carries out no input-output operations (network requests or the reading of files, for example).\\n\\n- Does not call `Math.random()` or `Date.now()` (since their result is unpredictable).\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\nThe advantages of pure functions:\\n\\n- Predictability: It is easy to understand what the function does.\\n\\n- Testability: No complex setup of the environment is required. It is enough to pass the arguments and check the result.\\n\\n- Reuse: They can be used safely in different parts of an application.\\n\\n- Caching: The result can be cached (memoized) on the basis of the arguments in order to avoid repeated computations.\\n\\nConclusion\\nStrive to write pure functions wherever that is possible, since they make code more stable and more comprehensible. Impure functions (for interaction with the DOM, the network or global state, for example) should be isolated and explicitly identified.","// A PURE FUNCTION\\nfunction add(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n// Always: add(2, 3) => 5. It changes nothing.\\n\\n// AN IMPURE FUNCTION\\nlet counter = 0;\\nfunction increment() {\\n  counter++; // A side effect: the change of an external variable\\n}\\n// AN IMPURE FUNCTION\\nfunction getRandomNumber() {\\n  return Math.random(); // The result is different on every call.\\n}\\n// AN IMPURE FUNCTION\\nfunction saveUser(user) {\\n  fetch(\'/api/users\', { // A side effect: a network request (IO)\\n    method: \'POST\',\\n    body: JSON.stringify(user)\\n  });\\n}"],[1163,"An `Object` is the basic \\"key-value\\" structure, in which the keys are usually strings or symbols. A `Map` is a more modern and more specialised \\"key-value\\" structure, which lets you use keys of any type (objects included), preserves the order of the elements and provides convenient methods for working with it. A `Set` is a collection of unique values of any type, which also preserves the order of the addition.","Every data structure is intended for its own tasks.\\nObject\\n\\n- The keys: Only strings or symbols. Numbers are automatically converted into strings.\\n\\n- The order of the elements: In modern JS the order of the enumeration for string keys corresponds to the order of the addition (with some nuances for integer keys), but historically it was not guaranteed.\\n\\n- The iteration: It is not iterable by default. To traverse it you have to use `Object.keys()`, `Object.values()` or `Object.entries()`.\\n\\n- The size: There is no built-in property for the size. It has to be computed through `Object.keys(obj).length`.\\n\\n- The use: It is ideal for the representation of structures whose keys are known in advance (configuration or a user\'s data with fixed fields, for example).\\n\\nMap\\n\\n- The keys: They can be of any type: objects, functions, primitives.\\n\\n- The order of the elements: It is guaranteed to preserve the order of the insertion of the elements.\\n\\n- The iteration: It is iterable. It can be iterated over directly by means of `for...of` or `forEach`.\\n\\n- The size: There is a built-in `size` property.\\n\\n- The performance: It is better suited for the frequent addition and deletion of key-value pairs.\\n\\n- The use: It is excellently suited for cases in which the key is unknown until run time, or in which the key has to be an object (for holding the metadata of DOM elements, for example).\\n\\nSet\\n\\n- The values: A collection of unique values of any type.\\n\\n- The order of the elements: It is guaranteed to preserve the order of the insertion of the elements.\\n\\n- The iteration: It is iterable.\\n\\n- The size: There is a built-in `size` property.\\n\\n- The use: It is ideal for the creation of lists without duplicates and for the fast checking of the presence of an element.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse an `Object` for static structures with known string keys. Use a `Map` when you need keys of a non-string type, when there are frequent updates, or when the order of the elements matters. Use a `Set` when you have to hold a collection of unique values.","const map = new Map();\\nconst keyObj = { id: 1 };\\n\\nmap.set(keyObj, \'value associated with object\');\\nmap.set(42, \'answer to everything\');\\n\\nconsole.log(map.get(keyObj)); // \'value associated with object\'\\nconsole.log(map.size); // 2\\n\\nconst set = new Set([1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4]); // The duplicates are ignored\\nset.add(5);\\nconsole.log(set.has(3)); // true\\nconsole.log(set.size); // 4 (the values: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5)"],[1164,"To optimise the logging of the cursor\'s coordinates you can use throttling or debouncing. Throttling guarantees that the function will be called no more often than once in a given span of time (every 100 ms, for example), which is ideally suited for the tracking of continuous movement. Debouncing postpones the call of the function until a pause in the events of a given duration has elapsed (250 ms, for example), which is better suited for the logging of the cursor\'s final position.","The `mousemove` event fires very often (dozens of times a second), which can create an excessive load.\\nThrottling\\nThrottling limits the frequency of the execution of a function. It guarantees that the function will be carried out no more than once in the period of time specified.\\n\\n- The principle: \\"Call the function no more often than once every N milliseconds\\".\\n\\n- It is ideal for: Continuous streams of events in which the regularity of the updates matters (the dragging of elements, the tracking of scrolling, the resizing of a window, for example).\\n\\nDebouncing\\nDebouncing postpones the execution of a function until the moment when a pause of a given duration occurs between the events.\\n\\n- The principle: \\"Call the function only if N milliseconds have elapsed since the last event and there have been no new events\\".\\n\\n- It is ideal for: Scenarios in which only the end point after a series of fast events matters (a search on input into a field, the validation of a form, the logging of the end of a resize, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: To optimise the tracking of the cursor, use throttling if you need regular updates of the coordinates during the movement. Use debouncing if what matters to you is to log the position in which the user stopped the cursor.","function throttle(func, delay) {\\n  let lastCall = 0;\\n  return function (...args) {\\n    const now = Date.now();\\n    if (now - lastCall < delay) {\\n      return; // We ignore the call if little time has elapsed since the previous one\\n    }\\n    lastCall = now;\\n    return func.apply(this, args);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst throttledLog = throttle((x, y) => {\\n  console.log(`Cursor at: ${x}, ${y}`);\\n}, 100); // We log a maximum of 10 times a second\\n\\ndocument.addEventListener(\'mousemove\', (e) => {\\n  throttledLog(e.clientX, e.clientY);\\n});\\n\\nfunction debounce(func, delay) {\\n  let timeoutId;\\n  return function (...args) {\\n    clearTimeout(timeoutId); // We reset the timer on every new event\\n    timeoutId = setTimeout(() => {\\n      func.apply(this, args);\\n    }, delay);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst debouncedLog = debounce((x, y) => {\\n  console.log(`Cursor finally at: ${x}, ${y}`); // Will fire after the cursor stops\\n}, 250);\\n\\ndocument.addEventListener(\'mousemove\', (e) => {\\n  debouncedLog(e.clientX, e.clientY);\\n});"],[1165,"Memory leaks in JS arise when objects that are no longer needed are not deleted by the garbage collector because references to them remain. To avoid that you have to: 1) Remove the event listeners from DOM elements when they are deleted. 2) Not hold references to DOM elements in global variables or closures for longer than is necessary. 3) Work carefully with closures and with large objects held in memory. 4) In modern frameworks (React, Vue), watch that you unsubscribe from subscriptions (setInterval, WebSocket) in the lifecycle hooks of the unmounting.","JavaScript uses the automatic management of memory by means of a garbage collector (GC). The GC deletes the objects to which there are no longer references from \\"live\\" objects.\\nThe widespread causes of memory leaks and their solution:\\n\\n- Forgotten timers and subscriptions:\\n\\n- The problem: `setInterval`, `setTimeout` or subscriptions to events (RxJS, for example) carry on working and holding references to objects even if the component that used them is no longer needed.\\n\\n- The solution: Always clear the timers and unsubscribe from the events when the component is destroyed.\\n\\n- Detached DOM elements:\\n\\n- The problem: If a reference to an element deleted from the DOM is saved in a variable, it will not be able to be collected as garbage.\\n\\n- The solution: Null the references to DOM elements after they are deleted.\\n\\n- Closures:\\n\\n- The problem: A closure may unintentionally retain a reference to a large data structure or a DOM element even if only a small part of it is used.\\n\\n- The solution: Be attentive to what your closures capture. After the use, null the variables referring to large objects.\\n\\n- Global variables:\\n\\n- The problem: Objects held in global variables will never be deleted.\\n\\n- The solution: Avoid global variables. Use local variables and modules.\\n\\n- Caches with no strategy of clearing:\\n\\n- The problem: A simple cache in the form of an object or a Map, which grows endlessly.\\n\\n- The solution: Implement strategies for the clearing of the cache (LRU — \\"Least Recently Used\\" — or a limitation by size, for example).\\n\\nConclusion: To prevent memory leaks, develop the habit of always freeing the resources (timers, subscriptions, references to the DOM) when an object or a component is destroyed. Use the developer tools (the Memory Profiler in Chrome DevTools, for example) to find and diagnose leaks.","// A React example with useEffect\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const intervalId = setInterval(() => {}, 1000);\\n  const subscription = someObservable.subscribe();\\n\\n  // The cleanup function\\n  return () => {\\n    clearInterval(intervalId);\\n    subscription.unsubscribe();\\n  };\\n}, []);\\n\\nlet element = document.getElementById(\'my-element\');\\n// ... the work with the element\\nelement.remove(); // We delete it from the DOM\\nelement = null;   // We delete the reference from JS"],[1166,"Conditional Types in TypeScript let you define types that are chosen on the basis of a condition checking other types. They are extremely useful for the creation of generic utilities that dynamically adapt to the types passed to them. The classic examples are the `Exclude<T, U>`, `Extract<T, U>` and `NonNullable<T>` types, and also the determination of the types of functions\' return values or of the types of properties in deeply nested structures.","Conditional types have the syntax `T extends U ? X : Y`, which reads as \\"if the type T can be assigned to the type U, then the resulting type will be X, otherwise Y\\".\\nThe main scenarios of application:\\n\\n- The creation of generic utility types:\\nMany of the utilities built into TypeScript are implemented through conditional types.\\n\\n- The overloading of functions in generics:\\nConditional types let you choose a type on the basis of the input generic.\\n\\n- The extraction of types from complex structures:\\nIt is often used together with `infer` to \\"pull out\\" a type from a wrapper.\\n\\nConclusion: Conditional Types are an advanced but incredibly powerful tool for the creation of flexible and expressive types, especially in libraries and complex code bases. They let types adapt to conditions, which minimises duplication and raises type safety.","// An example: the type Exclude<T, U> excludes from T every type that can be assigned to U\\ntype Exclude<T, U> = T extends U ? never : T;\\ntype T0 = Exclude<\\"a\\" | \\"b\\" | \\"c\\", \\"a\\" | \\"b\\">; // The result: \\"c\\"\\n\\n// An example: the type NonNullable<T> excludes null and undefined from T\\ntype NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;\\ntype T1 = NonNullable<string | null | undefined>; // The result: string\\n\\ntype ApiResponse<T extends { id: number }> = T extends { role: \'admin\' }\\n  ? T & { adminNotes: string } // If T has a \'role\' property with the value \'admin\', we add a field\\n  : T; // Otherwise we return the original type\\n\\ntype User = { id: number; name: string };\\ntype Admin = { id: number; name: string; role: \'admin\' };\\n\\ntype UserResponse = ApiResponse<User>; // { id: number; name: string }\\ntype AdminResponse = ApiResponse<Admin>; // { id: number; name: string; role: \'admin\'; adminNotes: string }\\n\\n// We obtain the type of a function\'s return value\\ntype MyReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : any;\\ntype Fn = (x: number) => boolean;\\ntype R = MyReturnType<Fn>; // type R = boolean\\n\\n// We obtain the type of an array\'s element\\ntype ArrayElementType<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : never;\\ntype Item = ArrayElementType<number[]>; // type Item = number"],[1167,"The Event Loop is the mechanism that lets JavaScript handle asynchronous operations despite the fact that the language is single-threaded. It constantly checks the queue of tasks and carries them out in order. First every synchronous operation is carried out, then the microtasks (promises), and only then the macrotasks (setTimeout, setInterval). This explains why some operations are carried out in a different order from the one in which they are written in the code.","The Event Loop is a fundamental mechanism of JavaScript, which ensures the execution of asynchronous code in a single-threaded environment.\\nThe main components of the Event Loop\\n\\n- The Call Stack — the stack of calls, where the synchronous operations are carried out\\n\\n- The Callback Queue — the queue of callbacks for the macrotasks\\n\\n- The Microtask Queue — the queue for the microtasks (promises)\\n\\n- The Event Loop — the mechanism that coordinates the working of all of the components\\n\\nThe priority of the execution\\n\\n- The synchronous code is carried out first and in full\\n\\n- The microtasks (promises) are carried out right after the synchronous code\\n\\n- The macrotasks (setTimeout, setInterval) are carried out after the microtasks\\n\\nAn example of the execution\\n\\nThe result in the console:\\n\\nThe explanation of the order of the execution\\n\\n- First all of the synchronous code is carried out: `console.log(\'1\')` and `console.log(\'4\')`\\n\\n- Then the Event Loop checks the queue of microtasks and carries out the promise: `console.log(\'3\')`\\n\\n- And only after that are the macrotasks carried out: `console.log(\'2\')`\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- The optimisation of performance — an understanding of the Event Loop helps you avoid blocking the main thread\\n\\n- Correct work with asynchronous operations — the predictable execution of code\\n\\n- The debugging of complex scenarios — an understanding of the order of the execution of the operations\\n\\nConclusion: The Event Loop is critically important for an understanding of asynchronicity in JavaScript. Use microtasks for high-priority asynchronous operations and macrotasks for operations that can be postponed.","console.log(\'1\');\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(\'2\');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n    console.log(\'3\');\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\'4\');\\n\\n1\\n4\\n3\\n2"],[1168,"The `this` keyword in JavaScript refers to the object in the context of which the function is being carried out. In non-strict mode, if a function is called without a context, `this` becomes the global object (window in the browser). In strict mode in such a situation `this` will be `undefined`. This helps you avoid accidental changes to global variables and makes code safer.","The behaviour of the `this` keyword is one of the most complex topics in JavaScript, and it differs substantially between strict and non-strict mode.\\nHow `this` is determined\\nThe value of `this` depends on the context of the function\'s call:\\n\\n- In an object\'s methods — `this` refers to the object itself\\n\\n- In constructors — `this` refers to the instance being created\\n\\n- In ordinary functions — it depends on the mode of the execution\\n\\nThe differences in strict mode\\nNon-strict mode\\n\\nStrict mode\\n\\nThe main cases of the determination of `this`\\n1. The call of an object\'s method\\n\\n2. The call of a constructor function\\n\\n3. The use of call, apply and bind\\n\\nThe practical recommendations\\n\\n- Always use strict mode in order to prevent errors\\n\\n- Use arrow functions when you have to preserve the context\\n\\n- Bind the context explicitly by means of bind, call or apply when it is necessary\\n\\nConclusion: Strict mode makes the behaviour of `this` more predictable and safer, preventing accidental references to the global object. That especially matters in large projects and when working with modules.","function showThis() {\\n    console.log(this);\\n}\\n\\nshowThis(); // Window (in the browser) or global (in Node.js)\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nfunction showThis() {\\n    console.log(this);\\n}\\n\\nshowThis(); // undefined\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n    name: \'Test\',\\n    showThis() {\\n        console.log(this.name);\\n    }\\n};\\n\\nobj.showThis(); // \\"Test\\" - in both modes\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'John\');\\nconsole.log(person.name); // \\"John\\" - in both modes\\n\\nfunction showName() {\\n    console.log(this.name);\\n}\\n\\nconst obj = { name: \'Alice\' };\\n\\nshowName.call(obj); // \\"Alice\\" - in both modes"],[1169,"Strict mode is a special mode in JavaScript, which makes code safer and prevents widespread errors. It forbids some syntactic constructs that are considered problematic and changes the behaviour of some operations. In strict mode, for example, you cannot use undeclared variables, delete non-deletable properties or duplicate a function\'s parameters. This helps you write better and more reliable code.","Strict mode represents a restricted version of JavaScript, which ensures better error checking and security.\\nThe activation of strict mode\\n\\n- For the whole file — add `\'use strict\';` at the beginning of the file\\n\\n- For a single function — add `\'use strict\';` at the beginning of the function\\n\\nThe main differences of strict mode\\n1. The handling of variables\\nNon-strict mode:\\n\\nStrict mode:\\n\\n2. The behaviour of `this`\\nNon-strict mode:\\n\\nStrict mode:\\n\\n3. The limitations on the syntax\\n\\n- The prohibition of the duplication of parameters\\n\\n- The prohibition of the use of reserved words as the names of variables\\n\\n- The prohibition of the deletion of non-deletable properties\\n\\nThe advantages of strict mode\\n\\n- Stricter error checking — many errors become exceptions\\n\\n- Improved performance — some optimisations are available only in strict mode\\n\\n- Better security — it prevents accidental actions with the global object\\n\\nThe practical application\\n\\n- Modern development — most frameworks use strict mode by default\\n\\n- Module systems — ES6 modules automatically work in strict mode\\n\\n- Code style and linters — they recommend that strict mode always be used\\n\\nConclusion: Strict mode should always be used in new projects, since it helps you avoid widespread errors and makes code safer and more predictable.","x = 10; // Creates a global variable\\nconsole.log(x); // 10\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\nx = 10; // ReferenceError: x is not defined\\n\\nfunction showThis() {\\n    console.log(this); // Window or global\\n}\\nshowThis();\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\nfunction showThis() {\\n    console.log(this); // undefined\\n}\\nshowThis();"],[1170,"In JavaScript there are several ways of creating objects. The simplest is to use the object literal `{}`. Constructor functions create objects through the `new` keyword. ES6 classes provide a modern syntax for the creation of objects. `Object.create()` lets you create objects with the prototype specified. Factory functions return new objects without the use of `new`. Every method has its own particulars of application.","The ways of creating objects in JavaScript\\nJavaScript provides several methods for the creation of objects, each with its own specifics:\\n1. An object literal\\nThe simplest and most widespread way:\\n\\n2. A constructor function\\nIt is used with the `new` operator:\\n\\n3. ES6 classes\\nThe modern syntax for the creation of objects:\\n\\n4. Object.create()\\nIt creates an object with the prototype specified:\\n\\n5. A factory function\\nA function returning a new object:\\n\\nConclusion: The choice of the method depends on the specific task — literals for simple objects, classes for complex hierarchies, factory functions for flexible creation.","const person = {\\n  name: \\"Ivan\\",\\n  age: 30,\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nfunction Person(name, age) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n}\\nconst person = new Person(\\"Ivan\\", 30);\\n\\nclass Person {\\n  constructor(name, age) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n    this.age = age;\\n  }\\n}\\nconst person = new Person(\\"Ivan\\", 30);\\n\\nconst personProto = {\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n  }\\n};\\nconst person = Object.create(personProto);\\nperson.name = \\"Ivan\\";\\nperson.age = 30;\\n\\nfunction createPerson(name, age) {\\n  return {\\n    name,\\n    age,\\n    greet() {\\n      console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n    }\\n  };\\n}\\nconst person = createPerson(\\"Ivan\\", 30);"],[1171,"A constructor function is an ordinary function that is used with the `new` operator to create objects. It defines the structure and the initial state of the objects created. When it is called with `new`, the function creates a new object, establishes its prototype and carries out the initialisation through the `this` keyword.","A constructor function in JavaScript\\nA constructor function is a special type of function, which is used to create and initialise objects.\\nThe definition of a constructor function\\nA constructor function is declared like an ordinary function, but it has particular conventions:\\n\\nThe particulars of constructor functions\\n\\n- The naming: By convention they begin with a capital letter\\n\\n- The use: They are called with the `new` operator\\n\\n- The return value: They usually return no value explicitly\\n\\n- The context: `this` inside the function refers to the object being created\\n\\nThe process of the creation of an object\\nWhen a function is called with `new`, the following takes place:\\n\\n- A new empty object is created\\n\\n- The object\'s prototype is established\\n\\n- The constructor function is called with `this` pointing to the new object\\n\\n- The object created is returned\\n\\nAn example of the use\\n\\nThe addition of methods to the prototype\\nFor the optimisation of memory it is better to add the methods to the prototype:\\n\\nConclusion: Constructor functions are used to create a multitude of objects with the same structure. They ensure the reuse of code and are the foundation for object-oriented programming in JavaScript prior to the appearance of classes.","function Person(name, age) {\\n  // The properties are established through this\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n  \\n  // Methods can also be added through this\\n  this.greet = function() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n// The creation of objects through the constructor\\nconst person1 = new Person(\'Ivan\', 30);\\nconst person2 = new Person(\'Maria\', 25);\\n\\nconsole.log(person1.name); // \\"Ivan\\"\\nconsole.log(person2.age);  // 25\\n\\nfunction Person(name, age) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  this.age = age;\\n}\\n\\n// The methods are added to the prototype\\nPerson.prototype.greet = function() {\\n  console.log(`Hello, I am ${this.name}`);\\n};"],[1172,"On the call of a constructor function with the `new` operator, the value of `this` is automatically set to the newly created object. That object is created implicitly before the constructor\'s code is carried out. Every property and method added through `this` becomes part of the object being created.","The behaviour of this in a constructor function\\nThe value of `this` inside a constructor function is determined by the way the function is called.\\nThe context of this on a call with new\\nWhen a function is called with the `new` operator, the following takes place:\\n\\nThe mechanism of the working of new\\nThe `new` operator carries out four steps:\\n\\n- The creation of the object: A new empty object is created\\n\\n- The establishment of the prototype: The object\'s prototype is tied to the constructor\'s `prototype` property\\n\\n- The binding of this: `this` is tied to the object created\\n\\n- The return of the object: If the function returns no object, `this` is returned\\n\\nThe difference from an ordinary call\\nWithout the `new` operator the behaviour of `this` differs:\\n\\nStrict mode\\nIn strict mode the behaviour becomes more predictable:\\n\\nThe checking of the call with new\\nTo protect against a call without `new` you can use checks:\\n\\nConclusion: In a constructor function `this` always refers to the object being created when it is called with `new`. An understanding of this mechanism matters for the correct use of constructors and for the avoidance of errors.","function Car(brand, model) {\\n  // this automatically refers to the new object\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n  this.model = model;\\n  this.year = 2024;\\n}\\n\\nconst myCar = new Car(\'Toyota\', \'Camry\');\\n\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n}\\n\\n// With new - this refers to the new object\\nconst car1 = new Car(\'Toyota\');\\nconsole.log(car1.brand); // \\"Toyota\\"\\n\\n// Without new - this depends on the context (undefined in strict mode)\\nconst car2 = Car(\'Honda\'); // An error or the addition to the global object\\n\\n\'use strict\';\\n\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n}\\n\\nconst car = new Car(\'Toyota\'); // It works\\nCar(\'Honda\'); // An error: Cannot set property \'brand\' of undefined\\n\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  if (!(this instanceof Car)) {\\n    throw new Error(\'The Car constructor has to be called with new\');\\n  }\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n}"],[1173,"On the call of a constructor function with the `new` operator, `this` is equal to the newly created object. If the function is called without `new`, the value of `this` depends on the context of the call — in strict mode it is `undefined`, in non-strict mode the global object (window in the browser).","The value of this on the call of a constructor function\\nThe value of `this` depends exclusively on how the constructor function is called.\\nThe call with the new operator\\nWith the correct use with `new`:\\n\\nThe call without the new operator\\nWith an ordinary call of the function:\\n\\nPractical examples\\nLet us look at various scenarios:\\n\\nProtection against an incorrect call\\nYou can add a check for safe use:\\n\\nThe modern approach with classes\\nIn ES6 classes a call without `new` always leads to an error:\\n\\nConclusion: `this` in a constructor function is equal to the new object only on a call with `new`. Always use `new` with constructors, or apply defensive checks in order to avoid errors.","function Person(name) {\\n  // this refers to the new object\\n  this.name = name;\\n  console.log(this); // Person { name: ... }\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  console.log(this); // It depends on the context\\n}\\n\\n// In non-strict mode\\nPerson(\'Ivan\'); // this = the global/window object\\n\\n// In strict mode\\n\'use strict\';\\nPerson(\'Ivan\'); // this = undefined (there will be an error)\\n\\n// Example 1: An ordinary call with new\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n  console.log(this instanceof Car); // true\\n}\\nconst car = new Car(\'Toyota\');\\n\\n// Example 2: A call without new (an error)\\nfunction Bike(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n}\\n// Bike(\'Yamaha\'); // An error in strict mode\\n\\n// Example 3: The call of a method as a constructor\\nconst obj = {\\n  createCar: function(brand) {\\n    this.brand = brand;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nobj.createCar(\'BMW\'); // this = obj\\nnew obj.createCar(\'Audi\'); // this = a new object\\n\\nfunction SafeConstructor(value) {\\n  // We check whether the function has been called with new\\n  if (!(this instanceof SafeConstructor)) {\\n    // If not, we call ourselves correctly\\n    return new SafeConstructor(value);\\n  }\\n  \\n  this.value = value;\\n}\\n\\n// Both calls work identically\\nconst obj1 = new SafeConstructor(\'test\');\\nconst obj2 = SafeConstructor(\'test\'); // It is corrected automatically\\n\\nclass Person {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\'); // OK\\n// Person(\'Ivan\'); // An error: Class constructor cannot be invoked without \'new\'"],[1174,"No, a constructor function does not always create an object. If the function is called without the `new` operator, no object is created. Also, if the constructor explicitly returns an object, then the object created automatically is ignored. A new instance is created only on a call with `new` and without the return of an object.","The conditions of the creation of objects by constructor functions\\nA constructor function creates an object only under particular conditions.\\nThe standard behaviour with new\\nWith normal use with the `new` operator:\\n\\nThe call without new\\nWithout the `new` operator no object is created:\\n\\nThe explicit return of a primitive\\nIf a primitive value is returned, it is ignored:\\n\\nThe explicit return of an object\\nIf an object is returned, it replaces the object created:\\n\\nThe conditions of the creation of an object\\nThe object is created when:\\n\\n- The function is called with the `new` operator\\n\\n- The function returns no object (or returns a primitive)\\n\\nA practical example with a check\\n\\nConclusion: A constructor function creates an object only on a call with `new` and when it returns no other object. An understanding of these conditions helps you avoid unexpected behaviour in code.","function Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  // We return no value explicitly\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\nconsole.log(person instanceof Person); // true - the object has been created\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n}\\n\\nconst result = Person(\'Ivan\'); // The call without new\\nconsole.log(result); // undefined - no object has been created\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  return 42; // The primitive is ignored\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\nconsole.log(person.name); // \\"Ivan\\" - the object has been created\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  return { custom: \'object\' }; // The object replaces the one created\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\nconsole.log(person); // { custom: \'object\' } - not a Person\\nconsole.log(person instanceof Person); // false\\n\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  // We check whether it has been called with new\\n  if (!(this instanceof Car)) {\\n    console.log(\'No object has been created - it was called without new\');\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  \\n  this.brand = brand;\\n}\\n\\nconst car1 = new Car(\'Toyota\'); // The object has been created\\nconst car2 = Car(\'Honda\');      // No object has been created"],[1175,"If a constructor function returns a primitive value (a number, a string, a boolean, null, undefined, a symbol), that return value is ignored. As a result the newly created object referred to by `this` is returned all the same. The `new` mechanism overrides the return of primitives.","The return of primitive values from a constructor\\nWhen a constructor function returns a primitive value, that does not affect the result of the creation of the object.\\nThe ignoring of primitive return values\\nEvery primitive type is ignored:\\n\\nPractical examples\\nLet us look at various scenarios with the return of primitives:\\n\\nA comparison with the return of an object\\nUnlike primitives, the return of an object changes the result:\\n\\nWhy primitives are ignored\\nThe mechanism of the `new` operator deliberately ignores primitive return values in order to:\\n\\n- Preserve the consistency of the creation of objects\\n\\n- Prevent accidental errors\\n\\n- Ensure the expected behaviour of constructors\\n\\nConclusion: The return of primitive values from a constructor function is ignored — the object created by the `new` operator is always returned. This behaviour guarantees that constructors always return instances of objects.","function Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  \\n  // All of these returns will be ignored\\n  return 42;                    // a number\\n  return \'a string\';            // a string\\n  return true;                  // a boolean\\n  return null;                  // null\\n  return undefined;             // undefined\\n  return Symbol(\'a symbol\');    // a symbol\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\nconsole.log(person.name); // \\"Ivan\\" - the object has been created\\nconsole.log(person instanceof Person); // true\\n\\n// Example 1: The return of a number\\nfunction Car(brand) {\\n  this.brand = brand;\\n  return 100; // Ignored\\n}\\n\\nconst car = new Car(\'Toyota\');\\nconsole.log(car.brand); // \\"Toyota\\"\\nconsole.log(car); // Car { brand: \\"Toyota\\" }\\n\\n// Example 2: The return of undefined (explicit and implicit)\\nfunction Bike(model) {\\n  this.model = model;\\n  // The implicit return of undefined - the object is created\\n}\\n\\nfunction Boat(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n  return undefined; // The explicit return - it is also ignored\\n}\\n\\nconst bike = new Bike(\'Yamaha\');\\nconst boat = new Boat(\'Ladya\');\\nconsole.log(bike instanceof Bike); // true\\nconsole.log(boat instanceof Boat); // true\\n\\nfunction TestPrimitive() {\\n  this.value = \'original\';\\n  return 123; // A primitive - ignored\\n}\\n\\nfunction TestObject() {\\n  this.value = \'original\';\\n  return { value: \'custom\' }; // An object - it replaces the result\\n}\\n\\nconst test1 = new TestPrimitive();\\nconst test2 = new TestObject();\\n\\nconsole.log(test1.value); // \\"original\\"\\nconsole.log(test2.value); // \\"custom\\""],[1176,"If a constructor function returns an object, then it is precisely that object that is returned, rather than the one created automatically through `this`. The object returned completely replaces the object referred to by `this`. This lets you create factory methods and control the process of the creation of objects.","The return of objects from constructor functions\\nWhen a constructor function returns an object, that radically changes the standard behaviour.\\nThe substitution of the object created\\nThe object returned replaces the object created by the `new` operator:\\n\\nA comparison with the ordinary behaviour\\nThe difference between the return of an object and of a primitive:\\n\\nThe practical application\\nThis behaviour is used in various patterns:\\n1. A factory method\\n\\n2. The caching of instances\\n\\n3. Decorators and wrappers\\n\\nThe limitations and the warnings\\nThe return of objects from constructors can be unobvious:\\n\\nThe modern alternative\\nInstead of returning objects from constructors it is better to use factory functions:\\n\\nConclusion: On the return of an object from a constructor function it is precisely that object that is returned, rather than the one created automatically. This is a powerful capability, but it should be used knowingly, since it violates the standard expectation of the behaviour of constructors.","function Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name; // This property will be lost\\n  \\n  // The object returned replaces the one created\\n  return { customName: \'A custom name\' };\\n}\\n\\nconst person = new Person(\'Ivan\');\\nconsole.log(person); // { customName: \'A custom name\' }\\nconsole.log(person.name); // undefined\\nconsole.log(person instanceof Person); // false\\n\\nfunction NormalConstructor() {\\n  this.property = \'a value\';\\n  // We return nothing (or we return a primitive)\\n}\\n\\nfunction ObjectReturningConstructor() {\\n  this.property = \'a value\';\\n  return { customProperty: \'a custom value\' };\\n}\\n\\nconst normal = new NormalConstructor();\\nconst custom = new ObjectReturningConstructor();\\n\\nconsole.log(normal.property); // \\"a value\\"\\nconsole.log(custom.property); // undefined\\nconsole.log(custom.customProperty); // \\"a custom value\\"\\n\\nfunction CreateUser(type) {\\n  if (type === \'admin\') {\\n    return { role: \'admin\', permissions: [\'read\', \'write\', \'delete\'] };\\n  } else {\\n    return { role: \'user\', permissions: [\'read\'] };\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst admin = new CreateUser(\'admin\');\\nconst user = new CreateUser(\'user\');\\n\\nfunction Logger(name) {\\n  // If the instance already exists, we return it\\n  if (Logger.instances[name]) {\\n    return Logger.instances[name];\\n  }\\n  \\n  this.name = name;\\n  Logger.instances[name] = this;\\n}\\n\\nLogger.instances = {};\\n\\nconst logger1 = new Logger(\'app\');\\nconst logger2 = new Logger(\'app\');\\nconsole.log(logger1 === logger2); // true - the same object\\n\\nfunction Observable(target) {\\n  const observers = [];\\n  \\n  return {\\n    subscribe: function(observer) {\\n      observers.push(observer);\\n    },\\n    notify: function(data) {\\n      observers.forEach(observer => observer(data));\\n    },\\n    // We preserve the original target\\n    target: target\\n  };\\n}\\n\\nconst observable = new Observable({ data: \'test\' });\\n\\nfunction ConfusingConstructor() {\\n  this.expected = \'the expected property\';\\n  return { unexpected: \'the unexpected property\' };\\n}\\n\\nconst obj = new ConfusingConstructor();\\n// A developer may expect obj.expected, but will get obj.unexpected\\n\\n// A more comprehensible alternative\\nfunction createUser(type) {\\n  if (type === \'admin\') {\\n    return { role: \'admin\', permissions: [\'read\', \'write\', \'delete\'] };\\n  } else {\\n    return { role: \'user\', permissions: [\'read\'] };\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// It is called without new\\nconst admin = createUser(\'admin\');"],[1177,"MVC, MVVM and MVP are architectural patterns for the separation of responsibility in applications. MVC separates them into the Model (the data), the View (the interface) and the Controller (the logic). MVP adds a Presenter as an intermediary. MVVM uses a ViewModel for the two-way binding of data. The main differences are in the way the components interact and in the level of coupling.","Architectural design patterns\\nThese patterns help you organise an application\'s code, separating the responsibility between various components.\\n1. MVC (Model-View-Controller)\\nThe structure:\\n\\n- The Model — the data and the business logic\\n\\n- The View — the display of the data, the user interface\\n\\n- The Controller — the handling of the user\'s input, the intermediary between the Model and the View\\n\\nThe interaction:\\n\\nAn example of the implementation:\\n\\n2. MVP (Model-View-Presenter)\\nThe structure:\\n\\n- The Model — the data and the business logic\\n\\n- The View — a passive interface, which delegates the actions to the Presenter\\n\\n- The Presenter — it obtains the data from the Model and prepares it for the View\\n\\nThe interaction:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The View is passive and knows nothing of the Model\\n\\n- The Presenter manages the View and handles the events\\n\\n- A closer coupling between the View and the Presenter\\n\\nAn example of the implementation:\\n\\n3. MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)\\nThe structure:\\n\\n- The Model — the data and the business logic\\n\\n- The View — the display, bound to the ViewModel\\n\\n- The ViewModel — the representation of the data for the View, two-way binding\\n\\nThe interaction:\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- The two-way binding of data (data binding)\\n\\n- The ViewModel knows nothing of the View\\n\\n- The automatic update of the View when the ViewModel changes\\n\\nAn example of the implementation (Vue.js):\\n\\nA comparison of the patterns\\nThe level of coupling:\\n\\n- MVC: The View knows about the Model\\n\\n- MVP: The View knows nothing of the Model, and is closely coupled with the Presenter\\n\\n- MVVM: The View knows about the ViewModel but not about the Model\\n\\nTestability:\\n\\n- MVP: The easiest of all to test (the Presenter is isolated)\\n\\n- MVVM: The ViewModel is easy to test\\n\\n- MVC: Harder to test because of the coupling\\n\\nThe application:\\n\\n- MVC: Traditional web applications (Ruby on Rails, Spring)\\n\\n- MVP: Applications with complex business logic\\n\\n- MVVM: Modern SPAs (Angular, Vue.js, WPF)\\n\\nConclusion: The choice of the pattern depends on the application\'s complexity, the requirements of testability and the team\'s preferences. MVC is suitable for simple applications, MVP for better testability, MVVM for applications with a rich UI and two-way binding.","The user -> the Controller -> the Model -> the View -> the user\\n\\n// Model\\nclass UserModel {\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.users = [];\\n  }\\n  \\n  addUser(user) {\\n    this.users.push(user);\\n  }\\n  \\n  getUsers() {\\n    return this.users;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// View\\nclass UserView {\\n  displayUsers(users) {\\n    users.forEach(user => {\\n      console.log(`User: ${user.name}, Email: ${user.email}`);\\n    });\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n// Controller\\nclass UserController {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n  }\\n  \\n  addUser(name, email) {\\n    this.model.addUser({ name, email });\\n    this.updateView();\\n  }\\n  \\n  updateView() {\\n    const users = this.model.getUsers();\\n    this.view.displayUsers(users);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nThe user -> the View -> the Presenter -> the Model -> the Presenter -> the View\\n\\n// View interface\\nclass IUserView {\\n  showUsers(users) {}\\n  getUserInput() {}\\n}\\n\\n// Presenter\\nclass UserPresenter {\\n  constructor(model, view) {\\n    this.model = model;\\n    this.view = view;\\n  }\\n  \\n  onAddUserButtonClick() {\\n    const userData = this.view.getUserInput();\\n    this.model.addUser(userData);\\n    this.updateView();\\n  }\\n  \\n  updateView() {\\n    const users = this.model.getUsers();\\n    this.view.showUsers(users);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nView <-> ViewModel <-> Model\\n\\n// ViewModel (the Vue instance)\\nnew Vue({\\n  el: \'#app\',\\n  data: {\\n    users: [],\\n    newUser: { name: \'\', email: \'\' }\\n  },\\n  methods: {\\n    addUser() {\\n      this.users.push({ ...this.newUser });\\n      this.newUser = { name: \'\', email: \'\' };\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\n// View (the HTML template)\\n/*\\n<div id=\\"app\\">\\n  <input v-model=\\"newUser.name\\" placeholder=\\"Name\\">\\n  <input v-model=\\"newUser.email\\" placeholder=\\"Email\\">\\n  <button @click=\\"addUser\\">Add User</button>\\n  <div v-for=\\"user in users\\">\\n    {{ user.name }} - {{ user.email }}\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n*/"],[1178,"JavaScript distinguishes between primitives (numbers, strings, booleans, null, undefined, symbol, bigint) and objects. Primitives are held by value, are immutable and are compared by value. Objects are held by reference, are mutable and are compared by reference. Primitives can temporarily behave like objects through wrappers.","Work with primitives and objects in JavaScript\\nAn understanding of the differences between primitives and objects is critically important for writing correct JavaScript code.\\nThe primitive types\\nJavaScript has 7 primitive types:\\n\\nObjects\\nEvery other value is an object:\\n\\nThe key differences in the behaviour\\n1. The storage in memory\\nPrimitives are held by value:\\n\\nObjects are held by reference:\\n\\n2. The comparison\\nPrimitives are compared by value:\\n\\nObjects are compared by reference:\\n\\n3. Mutability\\nPrimitives are immutable:\\n\\nObjects are mutable:\\n\\n4. The use of methods\\nPrimitives use wrappers:\\n\\nObjects use their methods directly:\\n\\nPractical examples of the differences\\n1. The passing into functions\\n\\n2. The copying\\n\\n3. The checking of types\\n\\nThe special cases\\nWrapper objects\\n\\nAutomatic boxing\\n\\nConclusion: Primitives and objects in JavaScript have fundamental differences in their storage, their comparison, their mutability and their behaviour. An understanding of these differences helps you avoid widespread errors and write more efficient code.","// The primitive values\\nconst number = 42;           // number\\nconst string = \\"hello\\";      // string  \\nconst boolean = true;        // boolean\\nconst nullValue = null;      // null\\nconst undefinedValue = undefined; // undefined\\nconst symbol = Symbol(\\"id\\"); // symbol\\nconst bigInt = 9007199254740991n; // bigint\\n\\n// The objects\\nconst object = { key: \\"value\\" };\\nconst array = [1, 2, 3];\\nconst functionObj = function() {};\\nconst date = new Date();\\nconst regex = /pattern/;\\n\\nlet a = 10;\\nlet b = a; // b receives a copy of the value\\na = 20;\\nconsole.log(a); // 20\\nconsole.log(b); // 10 - it has not changed\\n\\nlet obj1 = { value: 10 };\\nlet obj2 = obj1; // obj2 receives a reference to the same object\\nobj1.value = 20;\\nconsole.log(obj1.value); // 20\\nconsole.log(obj2.value); // 20 - it has changed too!\\n\\nconst a = \\"hello\\";\\nconst b = \\"hello\\";\\nconsole.log(a === b); // true - identical values\\n\\nconst num1 = 5;\\nconst num2 = 5;\\nconsole.log(num1 === num2); // true\\n\\nconst obj1 = { value: 10 };\\nconst obj2 = { value: 10 };\\nconst obj3 = obj1;\\n\\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj2); // false - different references\\nconsole.log(obj1 === obj3); // true - identical references\\n\\nlet str = \\"hello\\";\\nstr[0] = \\"H\\"; // It will not work\\nconsole.log(str); // \\"hello\\" - it has not changed\\n\\n// A new string is created\\nstr = str.toUpperCase();\\nconsole.log(str); // \\"HELLO\\" - a new primitive\\n\\nconst obj = { value: 10 };\\nobj.value = 20; // It changes the existing object\\nobj.newProp = \\"new\\"; // It adds a property\\nconsole.log(obj); // { value: 20, newProp: \\"new\\" }\\n\\nconst str = \\"hello\\";\\nconsole.log(str.toUpperCase()); // \\"HELLO\\"\\n\\n// What happens under the hood:\\n// 1. A temporary String object is created\\n// 2. The toUpperCase() method is called\\n// 3. The temporary object is deleted\\n\\nconst arr = [1, 2, 3];\\narr.push(4); // The method is called directly\\nconsole.log(arr); // [1, 2, 3, 4]\\n\\n// A primitive - it is passed by value\\nfunction modifyPrimitive(x) {\\n  x = x * 2;\\n  return x;\\n}\\n\\nlet num = 5;\\nmodifyPrimitive(num);\\nconsole.log(num); // 5 - it has not changed\\n\\n// An object - it is passed by reference\\nfunction modifyObject(obj) {\\n  obj.value = obj.value * 2;\\n}\\n\\nlet myObj = { value: 5 };\\nmodifyObject(myObj);\\nconsole.log(myObj.value); // 10 - it has changed\\n\\n// The shallow copying of objects\\nconst original = { a: 1, b: { c: 2 } };\\nconst shallowCopy = { ...original };\\n\\nshallowCopy.a = 10; // It does not affect original\\nshallowCopy.b.c = 20; // It affects original!\\nconsole.log(original.b.c); // 20\\n\\n// Deep copying\\nconst deepCopy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));\\ndeepCopy.b.c = 30;\\nconsole.log(original.b.c); // 20 - it has not changed\\n\\n// typeof for primitives\\nconsole.log(typeof \\"hello\\");    // \\"string\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof 42);         // \\"number\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof true);       // \\"boolean\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof undefined);  // \\"undefined\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof Symbol());   // \\"symbol\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof 42n);        // \\"bigint\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof null);       // \\"object\\" (a well-known mistake)\\n\\n// typeof for objects\\nconsole.log(typeof {});         // \\"object\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof []);         // \\"object\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof function(){});// \\"function\\"\\n\\n// The explicit creation of wrappers\\nconst strPrimitive = \\"hello\\";\\nconst strObject = new String(\\"hello\\");\\n\\nconsole.log(typeof strPrimitive); // \\"string\\"\\nconsole.log(typeof strObject);    // \\"object\\"\\nconsole.log(strPrimitive === strObject); // false\\n\\n// JavaScript boxes primitives automatically\\nconst str = \\"hello\\";\\nconsole.log(str.length); // 5 - a temporary String object\\n\\n// Equivalent to:\\nconst tempStr = new String(str);\\nconsole.log(tempStr.length);\\ntempStr = null; // The deletion of the temporary object"],[1179,"In JavaScript there are the primitive types (numbers, strings, boolean values) and the object data structures. The main structures include arrays for ordered collections, objects for holding key-value pairs, sets for unique values and maps for keys of any type. Every structure has its own methods and its optimal scenarios of use.","The primitive data types\\nThe basic primitives\\n\\n- Number: whole and fractional numbers\\n\\n- String: textual data\\n\\n- Boolean: the logical values true/false\\n\\n- Undefined: an undefined value\\n\\n- Null: the explicit absence of a value\\n\\n- Symbol: unique identifiers\\n\\n- BigInt: large whole numbers\\n\\nThe object data structures\\nArray\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- An ordered collection of elements\\n\\n- Indexing from 0\\n\\n- A dynamic size\\n\\n- Heterogeneous elements (of different types)\\n\\nObject\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A collection of key-value pairs\\n\\n- The keys are strings or Symbols\\n\\n- It does not guarantee the order of the properties\\n\\n- Prototypal inheritance\\n\\nSet\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A collection of unique values\\n\\n- The fast checking of the presence of an element\\n\\n- It preserves the order of the addition\\n\\nMap\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A collection of key-value pairs with keys of any type\\n\\n- It preserves the order of the addition\\n\\n- Better performance for frequent operations of addition and deletion\\n\\nThe specialised structures\\nWeakMap and WeakSet\\n\\n- Weak references (they do not prevent the collection of garbage)\\n\\n- The keys are objects only\\n\\n- They are not iterable\\n\\n- They are used for metadata\\n\\nArrayBuffer and TypedArrays\\n\\n- Binary data\\n\\n- A fixed length\\n\\n- They are used for work with files and the network\\n\\nA comparison of the structures\\nObject vs Map\\n\\n- Object: string keys, a prototype, JSON compatibility\\n\\n- Map: keys of any type, the preservation of the order, better performance for frequent changes\\n\\nArray vs Set\\n\\n- Array: duplicates are permitted, the order is by index, methods for manipulations\\n\\n- Set: unique values only, a fast search, simple operations\\n\\nConclusion: The choice of the data structure depends on the specific task — arrays for ordered data, objects for structured information, a Set for uniqueness, a Map for complex keys.","// The creation of and work with an array\\nconst fruits = [\'apple\', \'banana\', \'orange\'];\\nfruits.push(\'grape\'); // The addition of an element\\nfruits.pop(); // The deletion of the last element\\n\\n// The methods of an array\\nfruits.map(fruit => fruit.toUpperCase());\\nfruits.filter(fruit => fruit.length > 5);\\nfruits.reduce((acc, fruit) => acc + fruit.length, 0);\\n\\n// The creation of an object\\nconst person = {\\n    name: \'John\',\\n    age: 30,\\n    isStudent: false\\n};\\n\\n// The access to the properties\\nconsole.log(person.name); // John\\nperson.city = \'New York\'; // The addition of a property\\n\\n// The iteration over the properties\\nfor (let key in person) {\\n    console.log(key, person[key]);\\n}\\n\\n// Work with a Set\\nconst uniqueNumbers = new Set([1, 2, 3, 2, 1]);\\nuniqueNumbers.add(4); // The addition of an element\\nuniqueNumbers.has(3); // The checking of the presence - true\\nuniqueNumbers.delete(2); // The deletion of an element\\n\\n// The conversion into an array\\nconst arrayFromSet = Array.from(uniqueNumbers);\\n\\n// The use of a Map\\nconst userMap = new Map();\\nuserMap.set(\'id\', 123);\\nuserMap.set(42, \'answer\');\\nuserMap.set({}, \'object key\');\\n\\n// The retrieval of the values\\nconsole.log(userMap.get(\'id\')); // 123\\nconsole.log(userMap.size); // 3\\n\\n// The iteration\\nfor (let [key, value] of userMap) {\\n    console.log(key, value);\\n}"],[1180,"requestAnimationFrame is used for smooth animation and visual updates synchronised with the screen\'s refresh rate. It ensures optimal performance, automatically suspending the execution when the page is not visible and calling the callback before the browser\'s repaint.","The definition of requestAnimationFrame\\n`requestAnimationFrame` is a browser API that lets you carry code out synchronously with the update of the screen, usually 60 times a second.\\nThe main cases of use\\nThe creation of smooth animation\\n\\nThe optimisation of frequent visual updates\\n\\nThe advantages over setTimeout/setInterval\\nThe synchronisation with the browser\\n\\n- An automatic pause when the page is inactive or hidden\\n\\n- Agreement with the screen\'s refresh rate (usually 60fps)\\n\\n- The execution before the rendering — a guarantee that the data is current\\n\\nA comparison of the approaches\\n\\nPractical examples of the application\\nGame graphics and animations\\n\\nThe measurement of performance\\n\\nThe particulars of the working\\nThe time of the execution of the callback\\n\\nThe cancellation of an animation\\n\\nBest Practices\\nThe optimisation of performance\\n\\nThe batching of updates\\n\\nConclusion: requestAnimationFrame should be used for any visual updates and animations in which smoothness and performance matter. It ensures optimal synchronisation with the browser\'s rendering and adapts automatically to the system\'s resources.","function animateElement(element) {\\n    let startTime = null;\\n    const duration = 2000; // 2 seconds\\n    \\n    function step(timestamp) {\\n        if (!startTime) startTime = timestamp;\\n        const progress = timestamp - startTime;\\n        \\n        // The computation of the current position\\n        const translateX = (progress / duration) * 200;\\n        element.style.transform = `translateX(${translateX}px)`;\\n        \\n        // The continuation of the animation\\n        if (progress < duration) {\\n            requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    requestAnimationFrame(step);\\n}\\n\\nclass SmoothScroller {\\n    constructor() {\\n        this.isScrolling = false;\\n        this.targetScroll = 0;\\n    }\\n    \\n    scrollTo(target) {\\n        this.targetScroll = target;\\n        if (!this.isScrolling) {\\n            this.animateScroll();\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    animateScroll() {\\n        this.isScrolling = true;\\n        const currentScroll = window.pageYOffset;\\n        const distance = this.targetScroll - currentScroll;\\n        \\n        if (Math.abs(distance) < 1) {\\n            this.isScrolling = false;\\n            return;\\n        }\\n        \\n        // The smooth deceleration\\n        const newScroll = currentScroll + distance * 0.1;\\n        window.scrollTo(0, newScroll);\\n        \\n        requestAnimationFrame(() => this.animateScroll());\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Bad - with setTimeout\\nfunction animateWithTimeout() {\\n    element.style.left = (parseInt(element.style.left) + 1) + \'px\';\\n    setTimeout(animateWithTimeout, 16); // ~60fps\\n}\\n\\n// Good - with requestAnimationFrame\\nfunction animateWithRAF() {\\n    element.style.left = (parseInt(element.style.left) + 1) + \'px\';\\n    requestAnimationFrame(animateWithRAF);\\n}\\n\\nclass GameEngine {\\n    constructor() {\\n        this.lastTime = 0;\\n        this.isRunning = false;\\n    }\\n    \\n    start() {\\n        this.isRunning = true;\\n        this.gameLoop(0);\\n    }\\n    \\n    gameLoop(timestamp) {\\n        if (!this.isRunning) return;\\n        \\n        // The computation of the delta time for independence from the FPS\\n        const deltaTime = timestamp - this.lastTime;\\n        this.lastTime = timestamp;\\n        \\n        // The update of the game state\\n        this.update(deltaTime);\\n        \\n        // The painting of the frame\\n        this.render();\\n        \\n        // The next frame\\n        requestAnimationFrame((time) => this.gameLoop(time));\\n    }\\n    \\n    update(deltaTime) {\\n        // The update of the positions of the objects, the physics and so on\\n    }\\n    \\n    render() {\\n        // The painting of the game objects\\n    }\\n    \\n    stop() {\\n        this.isRunning = false;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction measureFPS() {\\n    let frameCount = 0;\\n    let lastTime = performance.now();\\n    let fps = 0;\\n    \\n    function countFrame() {\\n        frameCount++;\\n        const currentTime = performance.now();\\n        \\n        if (currentTime - lastTime >= 1000) {\\n            fps = frameCount;\\n            frameCount = 0;\\n            lastTime = currentTime;\\n            console.log(`FPS: ${fps}`);\\n        }\\n        \\n        requestAnimationFrame(countFrame);\\n    }\\n    \\n    requestAnimationFrame(countFrame);\\n}\\n\\nrequestAnimationFrame((timestamp) => {\\n    // timestamp - the time since the moment of the page\'s loading, in milliseconds\\n    // The precise time at which the rendering will be carried out\\n    console.log(`The frame will be painted at: ${timestamp}`);\\n});\\n\\nlet animationId = null;\\n\\nfunction startAnimation() {\\n    function animate() {\\n        // The logic of the animation\\n        animationId = requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n    }\\n    animationId = requestAnimationFrame(animate);\\n}\\n\\nfunction stopAnimation() {\\n    if (animationId) {\\n        cancelAnimationFrame(animationId);\\n        animationId = null;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction efficientAnimation() {\\n    let rafId = null;\\n    \\n    function animationStep() {\\n        // We carry out only the necessary computations\\n        performCalculations();\\n        \\n        // We check whether we have to continue\\n        if (shouldContinueAnimation()) {\\n            rafId = requestAnimationFrame(animationStep);\\n        } else {\\n            rafId = null;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    function start() {\\n        if (!rafId) {\\n            rafId = requestAnimationFrame(animationStep);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    function stop() {\\n        if (rafId) {\\n            cancelAnimationFrame(rafId);\\n            rafId = null;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    return { start, stop };\\n}\\n\\nclass BatchedAnimator {\\n    constructor() {\\n        this.callbacks = new Set();\\n        this.rafId = null;\\n    }\\n    \\n    addCallback(callback) {\\n        this.callbacks.add(callback);\\n        this.scheduleFrame();\\n    }\\n    \\n    removeCallback(callback) {\\n        this.callbacks.delete(callback);\\n        if (this.callbacks.size === 0 && this.rafId) {\\n            cancelAnimationFrame(this.rafId);\\n            this.rafId = null;\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    scheduleFrame() {\\n        if (!this.rafId) {\\n            this.rafId = requestAnimationFrame(() => {\\n                this.rafId = null;\\n                this.executeCallbacks();\\n            });\\n        }\\n    }\\n    \\n    executeCallbacks() {\\n        this.callbacks.forEach(callback => {\\n            try {\\n                callback();\\n            } catch (error) {\\n                console.error(\'Animation error:\', error);\\n            }\\n        });\\n    }\\n}"],[1181,"The Event Loop is the mechanism that lets JavaScript handle asynchronous operations in a single-threaded environment. It constantly checks the queue of tasks and carries them out in order. First every synchronous task from the call stack is carried out, then the microtasks (promises), then the macrotasks (setTimeout, events). This cycle ensures the non-blocking execution of code.","The main components of the Event Loop\\nThe Call Stack\\n\\n- A LIFO structure (last in, first out)\\n\\n- It holds the functions being carried out\\n\\n- One thread of execution — one operation at a moment in time\\n\\nThe Callback Queue\\n\\n- A FIFO structure (first in, first out)\\n\\n- It holds the asynchronous callbacks ready for execution\\n\\n- The macrotasks: setTimeout, setInterval, UI events\\n\\nThe Microtask Queue\\n\\n- It has priority over the callback queue\\n\\n- It holds the microtasks: Promise then/catch/finally, queueMicrotask\\n\\n- It is carried out in full after every macrotask\\n\\nThe principle of the working of the Event Loop\\nThe basic cycle\\n\\n- Carry out the synchronous code from the call stack\\n\\n- Check the queue of microtasks — carry out every one until it is empty\\n\\n- Carry out one macrotask from the callback queue\\n\\n- Repeat from step 2\\n\\nPractical examples\\nAn example of the execution\\n\\nA detailed analysis of the execution\\n\\nThe types of queue and their priorities\\nThe Microtask Queue\\n\\n- Promise callbacks (.then, .catch, .finally)\\n\\n- queueMicrotask()\\n\\n- MutationObserver (in the browser)\\n\\n- process.nextTick (in Node.js)\\n\\nThe Macrotask Queue\\n\\n- setTimeout, setInterval\\n\\n- setImmediate (Node.js)\\n\\n- I/O operations\\n\\n- UI rendering (the browser)\\n\\n- Events (click, load and so on)\\n\\nThe Event Loop in different environments\\nThe browser\\n\\n- Several queues for different types of event\\n\\n- Render steps — the update of the UI between the tasks\\n\\n- RequestAnimationFrame — the synchronisation with the frames of the animation\\n\\nNode.js\\n\\n- Several phases of the event loop: timers, pending callbacks, idle/prepare, poll, check, close\\n\\n- process.nextTick — a separate queue with the highest priority\\n\\n- setImmediate — the execution after the poll phase\\n\\nBlocking operations\\nThe problems of long computations\\n\\nBest Practices\\nThe avoidance of the blocking of the event loop\\n\\n- Split heavy computations into parts\\n\\n- Use Web Workers for CPU-intensive tasks\\n\\n- Apply asynchronous operations for I/O\\n\\n- Minimise synchronous operations in loops\\n\\nThe correct use of a Promise\\n\\nConclusion: An understanding of the Event Loop is critically important for writing efficient and responsive JavaScript code. Use asynchronous operations in order to prevent blocking and distribute the tasks between the queues correctly.","function first() {\\n    second();\\n    console.log(\'First\');\\n}\\n\\nfunction second() {\\n    console.log(\'Second\');\\n}\\n\\nfirst(); // Call stack: first -> second -> console.log\\n\\nconsole.log(\'Start\'); // 1. The synchronous code\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => {\\n    console.log(\'Timeout\'); // 4. A macrotask\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => {\\n    console.log(\'Promise\'); // 3. A microtask\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(\'End\'); // 2. The synchronous code\\n\\n// The result: Start, End, Promise, Timeout\\n\\n// 1. The synchronous phase\\nconsole.log(\'Script start\');\\n\\nsetTimeout(function timeout1() {\\n    console.log(\'Timeout 1\');\\n}, 0);\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(function promise1() {\\n    console.log(\'Promise 1\');\\n});\\n\\nsetTimeout(function timeout2() {\\n    console.log(\'Timeout 2\');\\n    Promise.resolve().then(function promise2() {\\n        console.log(\'Promise 2\');\\n    });\\n}, 0);\\n\\nconsole.log(\'Script end\');\\n\\n/*\\nThe order of the execution:\\n1. Script start\\n2. Script end\\n3. Promise 1        (a microtask)\\n4. Timeout 1        (a macrotask)\\n5. Timeout 2        (a macrotask)\\n6. Promise 2        (a microtask inside a macrotask)\\n*/\\n\\n// Bad - it blocks the event loop\\nfunction heavyCalculation() {\\n    let result = 0;\\n    for (let i = 0; i < 1000000000; i++) {\\n        result += Math.sqrt(i);\\n    }\\n    return result;\\n}\\n\\n// Better - split it into parts\\nasync function nonBlockingCalculation() {\\n    let result = 0;\\n    const chunkSize = 1000000;\\n    \\n    for (let i = 0; i < 1000000000; i += chunkSize) {\\n        // It lets the event loop handle other tasks\\n        await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 0));\\n        \\n        for (let j = i; j < i + chunkSize; j++) {\\n            result += Math.sqrt(j);\\n        }\\n    }\\n    return result;\\n}\\n\\n// The efficient creation of microtasks\\nasync function processData(data) {\\n    // Use queueMicrotask for high-priority tasks\\n    queueMicrotask(() => {\\n        console.log(\'High priority task\');\\n    });\\n    \\n    // A Promise for ordinary asynchronous operations\\n    return await fetch(\'/api/process\', {\\n        method: \'POST\',\\n        body: JSON.stringify(data)\\n    });\\n}"],[1182,"Fetch is a built-in browser API for carrying out HTTP requests. Axios is a third-party library providing a more convenient interface. Axios automatically handles JSON and errors, while Fetch requires more manual logic. Axios supports interceptors; Fetch does not. The choice depends on the project\'s complexity and the requirements of the network logic.","Fetch and Axios solve one task — the carrying out of HTTP requests — but they do it differently.\\nFetch\\nFetch is a native browser API.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- It is available without the installation of libraries\\n\\n- It returns a `Promise`\\n\\n- It does not count HTTP errors (`4xx`, `5xx`) as errors\\n\\n- It requires the manual parsing of the response\\n\\nAn example of the typical logic:\\n\\nAxios\\nAxios is an external library.\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- It parses JSON automatically\\n\\n- It throws an error on `4xx` and `5xx`\\n\\n- It supports interceptors\\n\\n- It is more convenient for large-scale applications\\n\\nThe practical difference\\nFetch is more often used:\\n\\n- in small projects\\n\\n- without complex authorisation logic\\n\\nAxios is more often chosen:\\n\\n- in an SPA\\n\\n- when there is authorisation\\n\\n- when there is complex handling of errors\\n\\nConclusion:\\nFetch is suitable for simple scenarios, Axios for large front-end applications with a shared network infrastructure.","fetch(url)\\n  .then(res => res.json()) // the checking of the status and the parsing by hand"],[1183,"The call stack is a data structure that holds information about the calls of functions. JavaScript adds functions to the stack when they are called and removes them once they have finished. The code is carried out strictly from the top down, on the LIFO principle. The call stack helps you track which function is being carried out at the given moment. An overflow of the stack leads to an error.","The call stack is the mechanism by means of which JavaScript tracks the execution of code.\\nHow the call stack works\\nJavaScript carries code out in the following way:\\n\\n- The global context is placed on the stack\\n\\n- On the call of a function a new context is created\\n\\n- The context is added to the top of the stack\\n\\n- Once the function has finished, the context is removed\\n\\nThis principle is called LIFO (Last In, First Out).\\nAn example of the logic of the execution\\n\\nThe order on the stack:\\n\\n- global\\n\\n- a\\n\\n- b\\n\\n- c\\n\\nWhy the call stack matters\\nThe call stack:\\n\\n- determines the order of the execution of the code\\n\\n- explains the synchronous nature of JavaScript\\n\\n- helps you comprehend errors and the stack trace\\n\\nThe typical problem\\nIf a function calls itself without an exit:\\n\\n- the stack overflows\\n\\n- `Maximum call stack size exceeded` arises\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe call stack is the foundation of the synchronous execution of JavaScript code and the key to an understanding of the order of the calls of functions.","function a() {\\n  b()\\n}\\n\\nfunction b() {\\n  c()\\n}\\n\\nfunction c() {\\n  // the execution\\n}\\n\\na()"],[1184,"The Web APIs are a set of APIs provided by the browser rather than by the JavaScript engine. They let you work with timers, HTTP requests and events. Asynchronous operations are carried out outside the call stack. Once they have finished, the result is returned back into JavaScript. This is the foundation of asynchronous behaviour in a browser.","JavaScript in itself cannot carry out asynchronous operations — that is the business of the Web APIs provided by the browser.\\nWhat counts as a Web API\\nThe Web APIs include:\\n\\n- `setTimeout`\\n\\n- `fetch`\\n\\n- DOM events\\n\\n- `WebSocket`\\n\\n- `localStorage`\\n\\nHow the Web APIs interact with JavaScript\\nThe process looks like this:\\n\\n- JavaScript passes the task to the Web API\\n\\n- The Web API carries the operation out asynchronously\\n\\n- Once it has finished, the callback is placed in the queue\\n\\n- The Event Loop returns the callback into the call stack\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe Web APIs:\\n\\n- unload the call stack\\n\\n- let asynchronous operations be carried out\\n\\n- make JavaScript non-blocking\\n\\nThe practical aspect\\nWithout the Web APIs:\\n\\n- HTTP requests could not be made\\n\\n- timers would block the execution of the code\\n\\n- the interface would freeze\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe Web APIs are the bridge between JavaScript and the browser\'s capabilities, which ensures the asynchronous working of applications.",null],[1185,"The task queue is the structure in which the callbacks of asynchronous operations land. The Event Loop watches the call stack and the task queue. When the call stack is empty, the Event Loop takes a task from the queue and places it on the stack. This is how JavaScript handles asynchronous code. It ensures non-blocking execution.","The task queue is an important part of the Event Loop mechanism, which is responsible for asynchronicity.\\nWhat the task queue is\\nThe task queue:\\n\\n- holds the callbacks\\n\\n- waits for the call stack to be freed\\n\\n- works on the FIFO principle\\n\\nHow tasks land in the queue\\nTasks are added:\\n\\n- after a `setTimeout`\\n\\n- after an HTTP request has finished\\n\\n- after the user\'s events\\n\\nThe role of the Event Loop\\nThe Event Loop:\\n\\n- Checks whether the call stack is empty\\n\\n- Takes the first task from the queue\\n\\n- Moves it onto the call stack\\n\\n- Starts the execution\\n\\nWhy this matters for the front end\\nAn understanding of the task queue helps you:\\n\\n- explain the order of the execution of the code\\n\\n- avoid race conditions\\n\\n- write predictable asynchronous code\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe task queue and the Event Loop ensure JavaScript\'s asynchronous model without the blocking of the main thread.",null],[1186,"Microtasks are carried out before macrotasks. First all of the synchronous code is carried out, then the microtask queue is emptied, and only then is the next macrotask taken. `Promise.then` and `queueMicrotask` count as microtasks, while `setTimeout` is a macrotask. This affects the order of the execution of code and the rendering. An understanding of the order helps you write asynchronous logic correctly.","The Event Loop carries tasks out not simply \\"in turn\\" but with a priority: microtasks have a higher priority than macrotasks.\\nWhat counts as a microtask and what as a macrotask\\n\\n- The microtask queue\\n\\n- `Promise.then / catch / finally`\\n\\n- `queueMicrotask`\\n\\n- `MutationObserver`\\n\\n- The macrotask queue (the task queue)\\n\\n- `setTimeout / setInterval`\\n\\n- UI events (`click`, for example)\\n\\n- network events (in the simplified understanding)\\n\\n- `postMessage` (often regarded as a task)\\n\\nThe order of the execution\\nIn one \\"cycle\\" of the Event Loop the typical order is this:\\n\\n- All of the synchronous code is carried out (until the call stack becomes empty)\\n\\n- Then every microtask that has accumulated is carried out\\n\\n- Then the browser may carry out a render (if it is needed)\\n\\n- Then the next macrotask is taken and the cycle repeats\\n\\nImportant: microtasks are carried out until the queue is completely empty. If more microtasks are added inside a microtask, they too will be carried out in that same pass, before the Event Loop moves on to the macrotasks.\\nA small example\\n\\nThe expected order:\\n\\n- `start`\\n\\n- `end`\\n\\n- `promise`\\n\\n- `timeout`\\n\\nThe practical significance on the front end\\n\\n- If you \\"overload\\" the microtasks (with chains of `Promise.then`, for example), you can:\\n\\n- postpone the handling of a `setTimeout`\\n\\n- delay the UI\'s reaction\\n\\n- worsen the application\'s responsiveness\\n\\nConclusion:\\nIn JavaScript, after the synchronous code the microtasks are always carried out first, and only then the macrotasks — this is the key to an understanding of the order of the execution of asynchronicity.","console.log(\'start\')\\n\\nsetTimeout(() => console.log(\'timeout\'), 0)\\n\\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\'promise\'))\\n\\nconsole.log(\'end\')"],[1187,"`setTimeout` does not guarantee the execution of the callback exactly after the time specified. It guarantees that the callback will not be carried out before the delay given has elapsed. Once the delay has expired, the callback lands in the task queue and will be carried out when the call stack is freed. The actual time is affected by the load, by blockings and by the priorities of the queues. The timer therefore often fires later.","`setTimeout(fn, ms)` is a timer with a minimal delay rather than a \\"precise alarm clock\\".\\nWhat setTimeout actually does\\n\\n- The browser starts a timer for `ms`\\n\\n- When the time has elapsed, the callback is not run straight away\\n\\n- The callback is placed in the macrotask queue\\n\\n- It will be carried out only when:\\n\\n- the call stack is empty\\n\\n- the microtask queue has been emptied\\n\\n- the Event Loop gets to that task\\n\\nWhy \\"exactly after ms\\" cannot be guaranteed\\nThe reasons for the delays:\\n\\n- long synchronous code is being carried out in the call stack\\n\\n- there are many microtasks (chains of `Promise.then`, for example)\\n\\n- the browser is busy with rendering or the handling of events\\n\\n- the tab is in the background (browsers may throttle timers)\\n\\nA minimal example of blocking\\n\\nEven with `0` the callback will be carried out after the `while` loop has finished.\\nIn practice\\n\\n- `setTimeout(..., 0)` does not mean \\"straight away\\" but \\"as it works out, when the queue gets there\\"\\n\\n- for precise intervals (for animations, for example) `requestAnimationFrame` is usually used\\n\\n- for \\"periodic tasks\\" with control of the drift, a scheduler of your own is often written\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`setTimeout` guarantees only a minimal delay, while the actual execution depends on the queues and on how busy the main thread is.","setTimeout(() => console.log(\'timeout\'), 0)\\n\\nconst start = Date.now()\\nwhile (Date.now() - start < 1000) {\\n  // we block the thread for 1 second\\n}"],[1188,"`setTimeout` can be carried out later because once the time has elapsed the callback lands in the task queue and waits its turn. If the main thread is busy with synchronous code, the execution is postponed. The delay is also increased by microtasks, the render and the handling of events. In the background the browser may deliberately slow timers down. The delay can therefore become noticeably greater than the value specified.","Even if a timer has \\"expired\\", the execution of the callback depends on when the Event Loop can actually carry the task out.\\nThe main causes of the delays\\n\\n- The blocking of the main thread (the call stack is busy)\\n\\n- long loops\\n\\n- heavy computations\\n\\n- synchronous operations that take a long time\\n\\n- The microtask queue has priority\\n\\n- after the finish of every task the Event Loop first carries out the microtasks\\n\\n- a large quantity of `Promise.then` can \\"push back\\" the macrotasks\\n\\n- The load: many tasks in the macrotask queue\\n\\n- a multitude of timers\\n\\n- event handlers\\n\\n- other callbacks that have accumulated\\n\\n- The render and the browser\'s working\\n\\n- the browser may insert stages of painting\\n\\n- heavy markup, styles or paint can affect the delays\\n\\n- The browser\'s limitations (throttling)\\n\\n- in inactive tabs timers are often \\"slowed down\\"\\n\\n- the saving of the battery and of resources\\n\\nThe practical conclusion for a developer\\n\\n- timers are not suitable for precise synchronisation\\n\\n- if precision matters, you have to:\\n\\n- measure the real time through `Date.now()` / `performance.now()`\\n\\n- take the drift into account and correct the schedule\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`setTimeout` is carried out later because the call stack is busy, because of the priority of the microtasks, the load on the queue and the browser\'s limitations — this is normal behaviour of the Event Loop.",null],[1189,"Primitive values in JavaScript are compared by value. For the comparison `===` is most often used, which takes both the type and the value into account. The `==` operator may carry out an implicit coercion of the types. For numbers, strings, booleans and `null`/`undefined` there are clear rules. An understanding of the differences matters for the avoidance of logical errors.","In JavaScript primitives are compared precisely by value rather than by reference.\\nWhich types count as primitives\\nThe primitive types include:\\n\\n- `number`\\n\\n- `string`\\n\\n- `boolean`\\n\\n- `null`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `symbol`\\n\\n- `bigint`\\n\\nThe operators of comparison\\n\\n- Strict comparison `===`\\n\\n- it compares the type and the value\\n\\n- it carries out no coercion of the types\\n\\n- it is the preferable variant\\n\\n- Non-strict comparison `==`\\n\\n- it carries out a coercion of the types\\n\\n- it can lead to unexpected results\\n\\nHow the comparison takes place\\nWith the use of `===`:\\n\\n- first the types are compared\\n\\n- then the values\\n\\nIf the types differ, the result is `false`.\\nWhy this matters on the front end\\n\\n- conditional renders depend on comparisons\\n\\n- errors in comparisons lead to bugs in the UI\\n\\n- `===` makes the code\'s behaviour predictable\\n\\nConclusion:\\nPrimitives in JavaScript are compared by value, and in most cases strict comparison `===` should be used.","1 === 1      // true\\n1 === \'1\'    // false\\n\\n1 == \'1\'     // true\\nnull == undefined // true"],[1190,"Objects in JavaScript are compared by reference rather than by their contents. Even if two objects have the same fields and values, they are considered different if they were created separately. The `===` operator checks whether the variables point to one and the same object in memory. Comparison by value through `===` is therefore impossible. This is a fundamental property of the language.","In JavaScript objects are reference types, and it is precisely that which determines the behaviour of the comparison.\\nHow objects are held\\nWhen an object is created:\\n\\n- it is placed in memory\\n\\n- the variable holds a reference to it\\n\\n- not the value itself but the address\\n\\nAlthough `a` and `b` look identical, they:\\n\\n- were created separately\\n\\n- have different references in memory\\n\\nHow `===` works for objects\\nThe `===` operator:\\n\\n- compares the references\\n\\n- does not analyse the object\'s contents\\n\\nBut:\\n\\nWhy JavaScript is arranged this way\\nThe reasons:\\n\\n- the comparison of references is carried out fast\\n\\n- deep comparison can be expensive\\n\\n- the language avoids implicit costs\\n\\nThe practical consequences\\n\\n- objects cannot be compared directly\\n\\n- changes to an object are visible to every reference\\n\\n- it matters to take that into account when working with state\\n\\nConclusion:\\n`===` for objects checks only the reference in memory rather than their contents, so comparison by value does not work.","const a = { x: 1 }\\nconst b = { x: 1 }\\n\\na === b // false\\n\\nconst c = a\\na === c // true"],[1191,"The comparison of objects by reference means the checking of whether the variables point to one and the same object in memory. If the references coincide, the objects are considered equal. If the objects have the same contents but different references, the result will be `false`. Such behaviour is characteristic of every reference type. It affects work with state and the comparison of data.","Comparison by reference is the checking of an object\'s identity rather than of its structure or its values.\\nWhat a reference is\\nA reference is:\\n\\n- a pointer to an object in memory\\n\\n- a way of accessing the data\\n\\n- not the object\'s value itself\\n\\nAn example of comparison by reference\\n\\nIn this example:\\n\\n- `obj1` and `obj2` refer to one object\\n\\n- `obj3` is a separate object with the same contents\\n\\nWhere this especially matters on the front end\\n\\n- in the comparison of `props` and `state`\\n\\n- in the optimisation of rendering\\n\\n- in the use of `useEffect` and `memo`\\n\\nThe typical mistake\\nTo expect that:\\n\\nIn practice this is always `false`.\\nConclusion:\\nThe comparison of objects by reference means the checking of whether it is one and the same object in memory, independently of its contents.","const obj1 = { value: 10 }\\nconst obj2 = obj1\\nconst obj3 = { value: 10 }\\n\\nobj1 === obj2 // true\\nobj1 === obj3 // false\\n\\n{ a: 1 } === { a: 1 } // true"],[1192,"Objects can be compared by value by means of the manual comparison of their fields, serialisation into JSON, or special functions of deep comparison. Third-party libraries are also used. Every way has its limitations of precision and of performance. There is no universal solution. The choice depends on the data\'s structure and on the task.","Since `===` compares only the references, additional approaches are used for the comparison of objects by value.\\nThe main ways of comparing\\n\\n- The manual comparison of fields\\nIt is suitable for simple structures known in advance.\\n\\n- specific properties are compared\\n\\n- the logic is easy to control\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- it does not scale\\n\\n- it is difficult to maintain when the structure changes\\n\\n- Shallow comparison\\nOnly the values of the first level of nesting are checked.\\n\\n- it is often used in UI optimisations\\n\\n- it is not suitable for nested objects\\n\\n- Serialisation through JSON\\nThe objects are converted into strings and compared.\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- the order of the keys matters\\n\\n- it does not work with functions, `undefined` or `Date`\\n\\n- it is slow for large objects\\n\\n- Deep comparison\\nEvery nested field is compared recursively.\\n\\n- the most precise variant\\n\\n- the most expensive in resources\\n\\nThe practical choice\\n\\n- a simple structure -> manual comparison\\n\\n- flat data -> shallow comparison\\n\\n- complex data -> deep comparison or a library\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe way of comparing objects by value is chosen on the basis of the data\'s complexity and the requirements of precision and performance.","a.x === b.x && a.y === b.y\\n\\nJSON.stringify(a) === JSON.stringify(b)"],[1193,"The deep comparison of objects is the comparison of every nested property and value. It checks whether the structure of the objects coincides completely. For this, recursion or the traversal of a tree is used. Such an approach gives a precise result, but it can be expensive in performance. It is used only where that is genuinely necessary.","The deep comparison of objects means the checking not only of the references or of the top level but of the whole data structure in its entirety.\\nHow deep comparison works\\nThe algorithm usually:\\n\\n- Compares the types of the values\\n\\n- Checks the primitives by value\\n\\n- For objects:\\n\\n- compares the number of keys\\n\\n- recursively compares every field\\n\\nThe simplified logic\\n\\n(The implementation is usually more complex and takes the edge cases into account.)\\nWhere deep comparison is applied\\n\\n- the comparison of complex state objects\\n\\n- the checking of changes in data\\n\\n- testing\\n\\n- the caching of results\\n\\nWhy this is not the default solution\\nDeep comparison:\\n\\n- requires recursion\\n\\n- traverses every nested level\\n\\n- can be expensive for large objects\\n\\nConclusion:\\nDeep comparison gives a precise result, but it should be used knowingly because of its cost in performance.","function deepEqual(a, b) {\\n  // the checking of the types and the primitives\\n  // the recursive traversal of the objects\\n}"],[1194,"Deep comparison can be slow, especially for large objects. It can loop endlessly when there are circular references. Difficulties also arise with types such as `Date`, `Map`, `Set` and functions. The frequent use of deep compare can worsen the UI\'s performance. It is therefore applied with care.","Despite its precision, deep comparison has a number of serious drawbacks, especially in front-end applications.\\nThe main problems of deep comparison\\n\\n- The problems of performance\\n\\n- the traversal of every nested level\\n\\n- a high cost for large objects\\n\\n- it can slow the rendering down\\n\\n- Circular references\\nObjects may refer to one another:\\n\\nWithout protection this will lead to infinite recursion.\\n\\n- Complex data types\\nProblems arise with:\\n\\n- `Date`\\n\\n- `Map`, `Set`\\n\\n- `RegExp`\\n\\n- functions\\n\\nThey cannot be compared correctly by a simple traversal.\\n\\n- The instability of references on the front end\\n\\n- new objects are created on every render\\n\\n- deep compare may always return `false`\\n\\n- it leads to superfluous updates\\n\\nThe practical approach\\nOn the front end people usually:\\n\\n- avoid deep comparisons at run time\\n\\n- use immutability\\n\\n- rely on comparison by reference\\n\\nConclusion:\\nDeep comparison is a powerful but dangerous tool, which can lead to problems with performance and stability if it is used without need.","a.self = a"],[1195,"Yes, third-party libraries are often used for the comparison of objects. They correctly handle nested structures and edge cases. Such libraries save time and reduce the number of errors. They are applied when a manual implementation is too complex. This is especially relevant for complex data.","In real projects developers often use ready-made libraries rather than writing a deep compare themselves.\\nWhy libraries are used\\nThe main reasons:\\n\\n- the correct handling of complex types\\n\\n- protection against circular references\\n\\n- the optimisation of performance\\n\\n- proven and tested implementations\\n\\nThe typical scenarios of use\\nLibraries for the comparison of objects are applied:\\n\\n- in tests (assertions on the equality of data)\\n\\n- in the comparison of complex state objects\\n\\n- in caching utilities\\n\\n- when working with configurations\\n\\nWhy not write it yourself\\nAn implementation of your own:\\n\\n- easily contains bugs\\n\\n- scales badly\\n\\n- requires maintenance\\n\\nEven a simple comparison quickly becomes complicated because of `Date`, `Map`, `Set`, arrays and nested structures.\\nAn important limitation\\nEven with a library:\\n\\n- deep comparison remains an expensive operation\\n\\n- it should not be used in hot places (the render, `useEffect`)\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThird-party libraries are justified when reliable and universal comparison of objects is required, but they have to be applied knowingly.",null],[1196,"A front-end developer can check an API straight from the browser. For that `fetch` or Axios in the DevTools console is used. Requests can also be checked through the Network tab. Sometimes temporary UI stubs are used. These ways let you check an API\'s behaviour quickly.","Postman is not the only way of checking the working of an API, especially for a front-end developer.\\nThe main ways of checking without Postman\\n\\n- Through the browser console\\n\\n- `fetch`\\n\\n- Axios (if it is attached in the project)\\n\\n- Through the Network tab in DevTools\\n\\n- the analysis of the real application\'s requests\\n\\n- the checking of the headers and the payload\\n\\n- the viewing of errors and timings\\n\\n- Through a temporary UI\\n\\n- a \\"check the API\\" button\\n\\n- a simple form\\n\\n- debug components\\n\\nWhy this is useful\\n\\n- it is closer to the application\'s real behaviour\\n\\n- the integration can be checked straight away\\n\\n- it requires no external tools\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- it is harder to manage a large quantity of requests\\n\\n- there is no convenient history or collections\\n\\nConclusion:\\nA front-end developer can efficiently check an API straight from the browser, using DevTools and the application\'s code.","fetch(\'/api/users\')\\n  .then(res => res.json())\\n  .then(console.log)"],[1197,"The `const` keyword forbids the reassignment of the reference to an object, but it does not forbid changes to its internal contents.\\nIf an object is declared through `const`, you cannot assign a different object to it, but you can change its properties.\\nThis is connected with the fact that `const` works at the level of the reference rather than at the level of the data structure.\\nThe addition, change and deletion of an object\'s properties is therefore permitted.","In JavaScript it matters to understand what exactly `const` does and at what level it works.\\nThe definition\\n`const` is a way of declaring a variable that forbids a change of the reference to the value but does not make the value itself immutable if it is an object.\\nHow this works\\nWhen you declare an object through `const`, the following takes place:\\n\\n- A reference to the object in memory is written into the variable\\n\\n- That reference becomes immutable\\n\\n- The object itself at that reference remains mutable\\n\\nWhy it was made this way\\nJavaScript separates:\\n\\n- the primitives (number, string, boolean)\\n\\n- the reference types (object, array, function)\\n\\nFor objects a change of the properties requires no change of the reference, and so `const` does not get in the way of it.\\nHow to make an object genuinely immutable\\nIf you have to forbid a change of the contents:\\n\\n- use `Object.freeze`\\n\\n- apply immutable approaches\\n\\n- create new objects instead of changing the existing ones\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`const` protects the reference rather than the object. For real immutability additional mechanisms have to be used.","const user = { name: \'Alex\' };\\n\\n// Permitted\\nuser.name = \'Bob\';\\nuser.age = 30;\\n\\n// An error\\nuser = { name: \'John\' };"],[1198,"`Object.assign` does the shallow copying of an object.\\n`JSON.stringify / JSON.parse` carries out deep copying, but with serious limitations.\\nWith the use of JSON, functions, `undefined`, `Symbol` and the special data types are lost.\\nJSON also cannot work with circular references.","Both approaches are used for the copying of objects, but they work in fundamentally different ways.\\n`Object.assign`\\nThis method copies properties from one object into another:\\n\\n- the copying takes place only at the first level\\n\\n- nested objects are copied by reference\\n\\n`JSON.stringify / JSON.parse`\\nThis way works differently:\\n\\n- The object is turned into a JSON string\\n\\n- Then the string is parsed back into an object\\n\\nThe particulars:\\n\\n- a new object is created at every level\\n\\n- this resembles deep copying\\n\\n- but only JSON-compatible data is supported\\n\\nThe key differences of the approaches\\nBefore the use it matters to take into account:\\n\\n- `Object.assign` is faster and safer for simple structures\\n\\n- the JSON approach breaks on complex data types\\n\\n- JSON does not support circular references\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`Object.assign` is shallow copying without surprises.\\nJSON copying is pseudo-deep, but with the loss of data and with limitations.","const original = { a: 1, nested: { b: 2 } };\\nconst copy = Object.assign({}, original);\\n\\ncopy.nested.b = 100;\\n// original.nested.b will also become 100\\n\\nconst copy = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(original));"],[1199,"`JSON.stringify` cannot work with every data type of JavaScript.\\nIt removes functions, `undefined`, `Symbol` and an object\'s methods.\\nIt also does not support circular references or special objects such as `Date` or `Map`.\\nBecause of that the result of the copying can differ from the original object.","Despite its popularity, JSON copying has many pitfalls.\\nThe loss of data types\\nOn the serialisation the following are lost:\\n\\n- `function`\\n\\n- `undefined`\\n\\n- `Symbol`\\n\\nProblems with the special objects\\nSome types are converted incorrectly:\\n\\n- a `Date` turns into a string\\n\\n- `Map` and `Set` are lost\\n\\n- a `RegExp` becomes an empty object\\n\\nCircular references\\nIf an object contains a reference to itself, an error will arise:\\n\\nThe loss of the prototypes\\nAfter the copying:\\n\\n- the object loses its prototype\\n\\n- the class\'s methods are not available\\n\\n- the object becomes an ordinary `Object`\\n\\nWhen this is critical\\nJSON copying is dangerous if:\\n\\n- the object contains business logic\\n\\n- classes or dates are used\\n\\n- the precise structure of the data matters\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\n`JSON.stringify` is suitable only for simple data.\\nFor complex structures it is better to use specialised methods of copying.","const obj = {\\n  a: 1,\\n  fn: () => {},\\n  value: undefined\\n};\\n\\n// fn and value will disappear\\n\\nconst obj = {};\\nobj.self = obj;\\n\\n// TypeError: Converting circular structure to JSON"],[1200,"Shallow copying is the copying of an object in which only its properties of the first level are copied.\\nIf a property contains a nested object, it is not the object itself that is copied but the reference to it.\\nAs a result the original and the copied object partially share one and the same data.\\nChanges in the nested objects will be visible in both copies.","Shallow copying is often used in JavaScript, but it can lead to unexpected effects if its limitations are not understood.\\nThe definition\\nShallow copying is copying in which:\\n\\n- a new object is created\\n\\n- only the values of the properties of the top level are copied\\n\\n- nested objects and arrays are copied by reference\\n\\nExamples of shallow copying\\nIn practice shallow copying is created by means of:\\n\\n- `Object.assign`\\n\\n- the spread operator `...`\\n\\n- array methods such as `slice`\\n\\nWhat happens in memory\\nAfter the copying:\\n\\n- `original !== copy`\\n\\n- `original.address === copy.address`\\n\\nThis means that the nested object is shared by both structures.\\nWhen this is fine\\nShallow copying is suitable if:\\n\\n- the object is flat\\n\\n- the nested structures do not change\\n\\n- an immutable approach is used (the creation of new nested objects)\\n\\nA brief conclusion\\nShallow copying creates a new shell of an object but does not duplicate the nested structures. This is fast, but it requires care","const original = {\\n  name: \'Alex\',\\n  address: { city: \'London\' }\\n};\\n\\nconst copy = { ...original };"]]');export{e as default};